hadiths[600]{hadithnumber,text}:
"6001","6398 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najdah narrated to us, he said: Baqiyyah narrated to us, he said: al-Zubaydi informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Idris al-Khawlani, from Abu Tha'labah al-Khushani: That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade every predator with fangs from beasts, and the flesh of domestic donkeys."
"6002","6399 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Suwayd narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Ubayd, freedman of Salamah ibn al-Akwa', narrated to me, he said: Salamah informed me that they were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the evening of the day they conquered Khaybar, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw fires being lit. So he said: What are these fires? They said: For the flesh of tame donkeys. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Pour out what is in them, and break them, meaning: the pots. Then a man from the people said: Or shall we wash them? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Or that."
"6003","6400 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Ubayd narrated to us, from Salamah, and he mentioned the like of it. So these reports had become successive from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the prohibition of eating the flesh of domestic donkeys. So the most deserving thing for us was to carry the hadith of Ghalib ibn al-Abjar upon what agrees with them, not upon what contradicts them. Then some people said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only forbade that to preserve riding beasts, not by way of prohibition. And they narrated in that [what follows]."
"6004","6401 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abbad ibn Musa al-Khuttali narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Umawi narrated to us, from al-A'mash, he said: I was narrated from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, he said: Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only forbade on the day of Khaybar eating the flesh of domestic donkeys because they were riding beasts."
"6005","6402 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed us, from Ibn Jurayj, that Nafi' informed him, from Abdillah ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade eating the domestic donkey on the day of Khaybar, and they had been in need of them."
"6006","6403 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Makki ibn Ibrahim and Abu Asim narrated to us, they said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Nafi' informed me, he said: Ibn Umar said, then he mentioned the like of it. So it was from the proof against them in that that Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, had informed that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fed them on that day horse flesh, and forbade them donkey flesh, while they were in greater need of horses than of donkeys. So his leaving preventing them eating horse flesh indicated that they were in sufficiency of riding beasts, and had they been in scarcity of riding beasts, such that for that they needed to be prevented from eating donkey flesh, they would have been in greater need of prevention from eating horse flesh, because they carry loads upon horses as they carry upon donkeys, and they ride horses after that for purposes for which they do not ride donkeys. So what we mentioned indicates that the cause for which they were prevented from eating donkey flesh is not this cause. And others said: They were only prevented on that day from eating donkey flesh because they were donkeys that ate filth. And they narrated in that what [follows]."
"6007","6404 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Shaybani, he said: I mentioned to Sa'id ibn Jubayr the hadith of Ibn Abi Awfa, regarding the command of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to them to overturn the pots on the day of Khaybar. So he said: He only forbade it because they used to eat filth. And they said: So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only forbade eating it for this cause. So it was from the proof against them in that that had there come in this only the command to overturn the pots, that would have borne what they said, but there has come this and there has come the prohibition in that unrestricted."
"6008","6405 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Shabbabah ibn Sawwar narrated to us, he said: Abu Zabr Abdillah ibn al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Mishkam, scribe of Abu al-Darda, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu Tha'labah al-Khushani saying: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, inform me what is lawful for me from what is unlawful upon me. So he said: Do not eat the domestic donkey, nor every predator with fangs from beasts. So the speech of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this hadith was an answer to the question of Abu Tha'labah to him about what is lawful for him from what is unlawful upon him. So that indicates his prohibition of eating the flesh of domestic donkeys, not for a cause that is in some of them to the exclusion of others, from eating filth and the like, but for them in themselves. And he, (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), placed it in his prohibition of it like the fanged predator from beasts. So just as the fanged one was prohibited, not for a cause, so likewise domestic donkeys were prohibited, not for a cause. And some people said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only forbade it because they were plunder. And they narrated in that [what follows]."
"6009","6406 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Harb ibn Shaddad narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from al-Nahhaz al-Hanafi, from Sinan ibn Salamah, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed on the day of Khaybar by pots in which was the flesh of the people's donkeys, and commanded regarding them and they were overturned. So it was from the proof against them in that that his saying: the people's donkeys, bears that they had plundered them from the people, and bears that they were attributed to the people because they ride them, so the prohibition fell upon them because they are domestic, not for other than that. They said: For there was narrated in that what indicates that they were plunder. So they mentioned what [follows]."
"6010","6407 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Adiyy ibn Thabit, from al-Bara, may Allah be pleased with him, that they obtained from the spoils donkeys and slaughtered them. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Overturn the pots. They said: So this hadith clarifies that those donkeys were plunder. So it is said to them: When it is established that they were plunder as you mentioned, what is your proof that the prohibition was for the plunder? And what made you, in interpreting that prohibition that it was for the plunder, more deserving than others in interpreting it that the prohibition of them was for them in themselves, not for the plunder. And we have mentioned in the hadith of Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: Overturn it, for it is filth, so that indicates that the prohibition fell upon them because they are filth, not because they are plunder. And in the hadith of Salamah ibn al-Akwa', may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: Overturn the pots, and break them. So they said: O Messenger of Allah, or shall we wash them? So he said: Or that, so that also indicates that the prohibition was for the impurity of donkey flesh, not because they are plunder, nor because they are usurped. Do you not see that had a man usurped from a man a sheep and slaughtered it and cooked its meat, that his pot in which he cooked that in it does not become impure, and that its ruling in its purity is the ruling of what was cooked in it of non-usurped meat? So what we mentioned of his commanding them to wash them indicates the impurity of what was cooked in them, upon that the command which was from him to throw away what was in them was for their impurity, not for their usurping it. And we have seen the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) command regarding a sheep that was usurped and slaughtered and cooked, contrary to this."
"6011","6408 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Nufayli narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, he said: Asim ibn Kulayb narrated to us, from his father, from a man, he said - I thought him from the Ansar, that he was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at a funeral, when the messenger of a Qurayshi woman met him inviting him to food, so we sat in the sittings of young boys apart from their fathers, then our fathers became aware of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and in his hand was a morsel, so he said: This is the flesh of a sheep informing me that it was taken without its due. Then the woman stood and said: O Messenger of Allah, it never ceased to please me that you eat in my house, and I sent to al-Baqi', and no sheep was found in it, and my brother had bought a sheep yesterday, so I sent for it to his family for the price, so he said: Feed it to the captives. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) refrained from eating it, and did not command throwing it away, but commanded them to give it in charity, when he commanded them to feed it to the captives. So this is the ruling of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding lawful meat, when it is usurped and consumed. So had the flesh of domestic donkeys been lawful with him, he would have commanded therein when it was plundered with the like of what he commanded in this sheep when it was usurped. But he only commanded regarding the flesh of those donkeys when he commanded, for a meaning other than the meaning for which he commanded regarding the flesh of this sheep what he commanded. Do you not see that had a man usurped from a man a sheep and slaughtered it and cooked its meat, that he is not commanded to throw that away in the saying of anyone from among the people, so likewise the meat of the domestic donkey slaughtered at Khaybar, had the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only forbidden it on account of the plunder whose ruling is the ruling of usurpation, then he would not have commanded them to throw away that meat, and would have commanded them therein with the like of what is commanded for one who usurps a sheep, slaughters it, and cooks its meat. So when it is negated that the prohibition of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of eating donkey flesh was for a meaning - from these meanings claimed by those who permitted its meat, it is established that his prohibition of it was for it in itself, like the prohibition of eating every predator with fangs from beasts, so that prohibition was for it in itself, so it is not fitting for anyone to oppose anything of that."
"6012","For the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let me not find one of you reclining upon his couch, when there comes to him a command from my commands and he says: Between us and you is the Book of Allah, so what we find therein of unlawful we prohibit, and what we find of lawful we permit; verily what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited is like what Allah prohibited. 6409 - Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us of that, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrated to us, from al-Hasan ibn Jabir, from al-Miqdam, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6013","6410 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Mushir narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hamzah narrated to us, he said: al-Zubaydi narrated to me, from Marwan ibn Ru'bah, that he narrated to him, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Awf al-Jurashi, from al-Miqdam ibn Ma'dikarib al-Kindi, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was given the Book and what equals it; soon a satiated one upon his couch will say: Between us and you is this Book, so what was therein of lawful we permit, and what was therein of unlawful we prohibit; verily it is not so; the fanged predator from beasts is not lawful, nor the domestic donkey."
"6014","6411 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Abu al-Nadr, from Abu Rafi', may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"6015","6412 - And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd informed me, from Abu al-Nadr, from Musa ibn Abdillah ibn Qays, from Abu Rafi', freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said while the people were around him: Let me not know one of you, when there comes to him a command from my commands, which I commanded or forbade, while he is reclining upon his couch, saying: What we found in the Book of Allah we acted upon, otherwise not."
"6016","6413 - Isa ibn Ibrahim al-Ghafiqi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn al-Munkadir and Abu al-Nadr, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi', from his father and others, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Let me not find one of you reclining upon his couch, when there comes to him a command from my commands, from what I commanded or forbade, saying: I do not know; what we found in the Book of Allah we followed. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) warned against opposing his command, just as he warned against opposing the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, so let one beware of opposing anything from the command of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), lest there befall him what befalls the opposer of the Book of Allah. And reports have become successive from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the prohibition of the flesh of domestic donkeys, with what we mentioned, and their meanings returned to what we described. So it is not fitting for anyone to oppose anything of that. So if one says: You have narrated from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, its permissibility, and what he argued with in that from the saying of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: {Say: I do not find in what was revealed to me anything forbidden to an eater who eats it} the verse. It is said to him: What the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said of that is more deserving than what Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said. And what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said of that is excepted from the verse; upon this ought to be carried what came from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with this successive coming, regarding the thing intended by itself, from what Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sent down in His Book as an unrestricted verse upon that genus, so what came from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of that is made excepted from that verse, not contradicting it, so that they do not oppose one another. And this is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may the mercy of Allah be upon them all."
"6017","12 - Chapter: Eating horse flesh. 6414 - Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Nu'aym narrated to us, (H). 6415 - And Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Dimashqi narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Abd Rabbihi and Khalid ibn Khali narrated to us, they said: Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from Thawr ibn Yazid, from Salih ibn Yahya ibn al-Miqdam, from his father, from his grandfather, from Khalid ibn al-Walid: That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the flesh of horses, mules, and donkeys. Abu Ja'far said: So some people went to this, and disliked horse flesh. And from those who went to that is Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him, and they argued in that with this hadith. Others disagreed with them in that, and said: There is no harm in eating horse flesh. And they argued in that with what [follows]."
"6018","6416 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Amr, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Ata ibn Rabah, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, he said: We used to eat horse flesh in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6019","6417 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us, from Abd al-Karim, and Waki', from Sufyan, from Abd al-Karim, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"6020","6418 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his wife Fatimah bint al-Mundhir, from Asma bint Abi Bakr, she said: We slaughtered a horse in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and ate it. And in this chapter are reports which have entered in the chapter of the prohibition of the flesh of domestic donkeys, so that sufficed us from repeating them. So some people went to these reports, and permitted eating horse flesh, and from those who went to that are Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them both, and they argued in that with the succession of the reports in that and their concurrence. And had that been taken by way of reasoning, there would have been no difference between domestic horses and domestic donkeys. But the reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when they are sound and successive, are more deserving to be said by than reasoning, especially when Jabir ibn Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with them both, had informed in his hadith that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted them horse flesh at the time of his preventing them from the flesh of domestic donkeys, so that indicates the difference of the ruling of their flesh."
"6021","25 - Book of Drinks. 1 - Chapter: What is the prohibited wine? 6419 - Abu Bakrah Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Kathir, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Wine is from these two trees: the date-palm and the grapevine."
"6022","6420 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from al-Awza'i and Ikrimah ibn Ammar, from Abu Kathir, and Hisham from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Kathir, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"6023","6421 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Humran narrated to us, he said: Uqbah ibn al-Taw'am al-Raqashi narrated to us, he said: Abu Kathir al-Yamami narrated to me, he said: I entered from al-Yamamah to Madinah, when the people increased disputing about nabidh, to meet Abu Hurayrah and ask him about that, so I met him and said: O Abu Hurayrah, I came to you from al-Yamamah asking you about nabidh, so narrate to me from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), do not narrate to me from others. So he said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Wine is from the grapevine and the date-palm. Abu Ja'far said: So some people went to that wine is from dates and grapes together, and argued in that with this hadith. Others disagreed with them in that and said: The prohibited wine in the Book of Allah, Exalted, is the wine which is from grape juice when the juice ferments and throws up foam; thus Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him, used to say. And Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on him, said: When it ferments, even if it does not throw up foam, it has become wine. And the hadith which we narrated from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at the beginning of this chapter, is not contrary to that with us, because it bears that he meant by his saying: Wine is from these two trees, one of them, so he addressed them both together while meaning one of them to the exclusion of the other, just as Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: {There come forth from them pearl and coral} while it only comes forth from one of them. And just as He said: {O company of jinn and men, did there not come to you messengers from among you} while the messengers are from men, not from jinn."
"6024","And just as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, in the hadith of Ubadah ibn al-Samit: When he took upon his companions in the pledge as he took upon the women that you do not associate, nor steal, nor commit adultery. Then he said: Whoever commits something of that and is punished for it, it is expiation for him. 6422 - Yunus narrated to us of that, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Idris, from Ubadah ibn al-Samit, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And we knew one who associated, and was punished for his association, that is not expiation. So what we mentioned indicates that he only meant what is besides association, from what he mentioned in this hadith. So when these things had come, their outward upon جمع, and their inward upon a specific part of that, it also bears that his saying: Wine is from these two trees, the date-palm and the grapevine, its outward is upon them both, and its inward upon one of them, so the wine intended in that is from the grapevine, not from the date-palm. And his saying: Wine is from these two trees also bears that he meant both trees together, and that what intoxicates from their fruits is wine, as Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad went to regarding what is soaked of raisins and dates, so they made it wine. And his saying: Wine is from these two trees bears that he meant wine from them both, though different, upon that it is from grapes what we knew of wine, and upon that it is from dates what intoxicates, so the wine of grapes is the very juice when it becomes strong, and the wine of dates is the amount of date nabidh which intoxicates. So when this hadith bore these aspects which we mentioned, none of them was more deserving than the rest, and no interpreter could interpret it upon one of them except that his opponent could interpret it upon that. So if one says: What is the meaning of the hadith of Umar? Meaning what [follows]."
"6025","6423 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Numayr narrated to us, he said: I heard Ibn Idris, he said: I heard Abu Hayyan al-Taymi, from al-Sha'bi, from Ibn Umar, he said: I heard Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, upon the pulpit of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: To proceed, O people, the prohibition of wine came down, while it on that day was from five: dates, grapes, honey, wheat, and barley; and wine is what covers the mind. And the like of that was also narrated from Ibn Umar and al-Nu'man, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6026","6424 - Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Aswad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Abu al-Nadr, from Salim ibn Abdillah, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: From grapes is wine, and I forbid you every intoxicant."
"6027","6425 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, from Isra'il, from Ibrahim ibn al-Muhajir, from al-Sha'bi, from al-Nu'man ibn Bashir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it, except that he did not mention his saying: And I forbid you every intoxicant. It is said to him: These two hadiths bear all the meanings which the first hadith bears, except one meaning, and it is what the first hadith bore from what those who went to the dislike of date and raisin soak were carried upon, for this hadith does not bear it, because it joined with that the wine of wheat and the wine of barley, while they do not say that, because they see no harm in the soak of wheat and barley, and they differentiate between them and between the soak of dates and raisins, so that interpretation this hadith does not bear, but it bears the other interpretations just as the first hadith bears them. So if one argues in that with what was narrated from Anas, and it is what [follows]."
"6028","6426 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq al-Hamdani narrated to us, from Burayd ibn Abi Maryam, from Anas, he said: We in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to soak fresh and unripe dates, then when the prohibition of wine came down we poured them out of the vessels, then left them."
"6029","6427 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ja'far narrated to us, he said: Humayd al-Tawil narrated to us, from Anas, he said: Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah, Suhayl ibn al-Bayda, and Ubayy ibn Ka'b were with Abu Talhah while I was serving them drink, until it almost took effect in them. He said: Then a passerby from among the Muslims passed by us and called: Have you not known? Wine has been prohibited; by Allah, they did not wait for them to command me to throw what was in the vessels, so I did, and they did not return to anything of it until they met Allah, and it was unripe and ripe dates, and it was our wine on that day."
"6030","6428 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: Humayd narrated to us, from Anas, the like of it."
"6031","6429 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: Thabit and Humayd informed me, from Anas, he said: I was serving Abu Talhah, Suhayl ibn Bayda, Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah, and Abu Dujanah a mixture of unripe and ripe dates, until it began to take effect in them, when a man called: Verily wine has been prohibited; by Allah, they did not wait until they knew whether what he said was true or false, so they said: Pour out your vessel, O Anas, and I poured it out, and it did not return to their heads until they met Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and their wine on that day was unripe and ripe dates."
"6032","6430 - Abdillah ibn Muhammad ibn Khushaysh narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas, he said: I was serving Abu Talhah, Abu Dujanah, and Suhayl ibn Bayda a mixture of unripe dates and dates, when wine was prohibited, so I poured it out while I was their cupbearer on that day and the youngest of them, and we counted it on that day as wine. They said: This is what indicates that that was also wine. It is said to them: There is no proof in that for what you mentioned, because it is possible that the drink was a fermented date soak, so the saying of one who disliked date soak is established by that, but the proof of the unlawfulness of its cooked form is not necessitated by that. And it bears that they did that because they knew that much of that intoxicates, so they did not feel safe for themselves falling into it, due to their recent acquaintance with it, so they broke it for that. And as for the saying of Anas: And it was our wine on that day, it bears that he meant by that: What we used to ferment. And the proof for that is what [follows]."
"6033","6431 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Shihab narrated to us, from Abu Layla, from Isa, that his father sent him to Anas for a need, and he saw with him a strong tila, and tila is what much of it intoxicates, yet that with Anas was not wine, though much of it intoxicates. And it is established by what we described that wine with Anas was not from every drink, but from a specific part of drinks. And we found from the reports what also indicates what we mentioned, from what we interpreted the hadiths of Anas upon. And from that is [what follows]."
"6034","6432 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Mis'ar ibn Kidam narrated to us, from Abu Awn al-Thaqafi, from Abdillah ibn Shaddad ibn al-Had, from Abdillah ibn Abbas, he said: Wine was prohibited by itself, and intoxication from every drink. So Ibn Abbas informed that the prohibition fell upon wine by itself, and upon intoxication from the rest of drinks besides it. So it is established by that that what is besides the wine which was prohibited, from what much of it intoxicates, the drinking of its little which does not intoxicate was permitted, upon what it was of prior permissibility before the prohibition of wine, and that the new prohibition is only in the very wine and intoxication from what is in other than it of drinks. So it bore that the prohibited wine is grape juice specifically, and bore that it is everything that ferments, from grape juice and others. So when that bore that, and things had preceded as lawful altogether, then prohibition occurred in some of them, nothing from what had been agreed upon its lawfulness is removed except by an agreement coming upon its prohibition. And we testify upon Allah, Mighty and Majestic, that He prohibited grape juice when the qualities of wine occur in it, and we do not testify upon Him that He prohibited what is besides that when the like of this quality occurs in it. So what we testify upon Allah of His prohibiting it is the wine whose interpretation we believed in, from where we believed in its revelation. And what we do not testify upon Allah that He prohibited is the drink which is not wine. So what was of wine, its little and much is unlawful, and what was from what is besides that of drinks, intoxication from it is unlawful, and what is besides that of it is permissible. This is the reasoning with us, and it is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them. Except the soak of raisins and dates specifically, for they disliked it [intensely, as follows]."
"6035","6433 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Awn narrated to us, he said: Hushaym informed us, from Ibn Shubrumah, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, that he said in that: It is wine, so avoid it."
"6036","2 - Chapter: What is prohibited of nabidh. 6434 - Yazid ibn Sinan and Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, they said: Abdillah ibn Maslamah narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Umar narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad, from Muslim ibn Yasar, from Sufyan ibn Wahb al-Khawlani, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every intoxicant is unlawful."
"6037","6435 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr informed me, from Abu Salamah, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every intoxicant is wine, and every intoxicant is unlawful."
"6038","6436 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr informed me, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"6039","6437 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adiyy informed me, he said: Abdillah ibn Idris narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah and Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"6040","6438 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Rabi' al-Zahrani informed me, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd informed me, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"6041","6439 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Khattab ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Majid narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"6042","6440 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed me, he said: Ibn Ajlan narrated to me, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"6043","6441 - Muhammad ibn Idris al-Makki narrated to us, he said: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"6044","6442 - Muhammad ibn Idris al-Makki narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it, and did not raise it."
"6045","6443 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ja'far informed me, he said: al-Dahhak ibn Uthman informed me, from Bukayr ibn Abdillah ibn al-Ashajj, from Amir ibn Sa'd, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I forbid you the little of what much of it intoxicates."
"6046","6444 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad al-Muharibi informed me, from al-Hasan ibn Amr al-Fuqaymi, from al-Hakam, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Umm Salamah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade every intoxicant."
"6047","6445 - Yunus and Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, they said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Amr, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Qays ibn Habtar, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, prohibited wine, gambling, and the kuba, and said: Every intoxicant is unlawful."
"6048","6446 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Isa narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, he said: Ibn Shihab al-Zuhri narrated to us, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about bit' and said: Every drink that intoxicates is unlawful."
"6049","6447 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me, he said: Malik and Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"6050","6448 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Surayj ibn al-Nu'man al-Jawhari narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Every drink that intoxicates is unlawful."
"6051","6449 - Ali narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Mahdi ibn Maymun narrated to us, from Abu Uthman al-Ansari, he said: I heard al-Qasim ibn Muhammad narrating from Aishah, she said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Every intoxicant is unlawful, and what a faraq of it intoxicates, a handful of it is unlawful."
"6052","6450 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Abdillah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Maymunah, and from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Aishah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Every drink that intoxicates is unlawful."
"6053","6451 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Walid ibn Abdah, from Abdillah ibn Amr, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade wine, gambling, and the kuba, and said: Every intoxicant is unlawful."
"6054","6452 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from Abdillah ibn Amr, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What much of it intoxicates, its little is unlawful."
"6055","6453 - Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Aswad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah informed me, from Abu Hubayrah, he said: I heard an elder narrating to Abu Tamim that he heard Qays ibn Sa'd ibn Ubadah upon the pulpit saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Every intoxicant is unlawful."
"6056","6454 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ya'la ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ja'far informed me, from Dawud ibn Bakr, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What much of it intoxicates, its little is unlawful."
"6057","6455 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, from Uthman ibn Matar, from Abu Hariz, from al-Sha'bi, he said: I heard al-Nu'man ibn Bashir saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I forbid you every intoxicant."
"6058","6456 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Bahr narrated to us, he said: Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: I read upon Fudayl ibn Maysarah Abu Mu'adh, he said: Abu Hariz narrated to me, that al-Sha'bi narrated to him, he said: I heard al-Nu'man ibn Bashir delivering a sermon upon the pulpit of Kufah, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I forbid you every intoxicant."
"6059","6457 - Mubashshir ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: al-Huraysh ibn Muslim al-Kufi narrated to us, from Talhah al-Yamami, from Abu Burdah, from Abu Musa, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every intoxicant is unlawful."
"6060","6458 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Abi Burdah, he said: I heard my father narrating from Abu Musa that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he sent Abu Musa and Mu'adh to Yemen, Abu Musa said: A drink is made in our land from honey, called bit', and from barley, called mizr. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every intoxicant is unlawful. Abu Ja'far said: So some people went to prohibiting the little and much of nabidh, and argued in that with these reports. Others disagreed with them in that, and permitted of that what does not intoxicate, and prohibited the much which intoxicates. And it was from the proof for them in that that these reports which we mentioned were narrated from a group of the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). But their interpretation bears to be as those who prohibited the little and much of nabidh went to, and bears to be upon the amount from which its drinker becomes intoxicated specifically. So when these reports bore each one of these two interpretations, we examined what is besides them, that it be known by it which of the two meanings was intended by what we mentioned in them. So we found Umar ibn al-Khattab, and he is one of the men from whom we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Every intoxicant is unlawful. There was narrated from him regarding the permissibility of the little of strong nabidh what [follows]."
"6061","6459 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: al-A'mash narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim narrated to me, from Hammam ibn al-Harith, from Umar, that he was on a journey, and nabidh was brought to him, so he drank from it and frowned, then said: The nabidh of Ta'if has strong effect, and he mentioned a severity I do not remember, then he called for water and it was poured upon it, then he drank."
"6062","6460 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Maymun, he said: I witnessed Umar when he was stabbed, and the physician came to him and said: Which drink is most beloved to you? He said: Nabidh, so nabidh was brought and he drank from it, and it came out of one of his wounds."
"6063","6461 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from Amr ibn Maymun, the like of it, and added: Umar said, and he used to say: We drink from this nabidh a drink that cuts camel meat in our stomachs lest it harm us, he said: And I drank from his nabidh, and it was the strongest nabidh."
"6064","6462 - Rawh narrated to us, he said: Amr narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq said, from Amir, from Sa'id ibn Dhi La'wah, he said: A drunken man was brought to Umar, and he flogged him, so he said: I only drank from your drink, so he said: Even so."
"6065","6463 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from al-A'mash, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to me, from Sa'id ibn Dhi Huddan, or Ibn Dhi La'wah, he said: A thirsty man came to the treasurer of Umar and asked him for drink, but he did not give him, so he was brought a flat vessel of Umar's, and drank from it and became intoxicated, so he was brought to Umar and excused himself to him and said: I only drank from your flat vessel. So Umar said: I only strike you for intoxication, and Umar struck him."
"6066","6464 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from al-A'mash, he said: Habib ibn Abi Thabit narrated to me, from Nafi', from Ibn Alqamah, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab commanded nabidh of his to be made in one of those houses, but it was delayed for them one night, so food was brought and he ate, then nabidh was brought that had become old and strong, so he drank from it, then said: This is strong, then he commanded water and it was poured upon it, then he and his companions drank."
"6067","6465 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: Khalid al-Hadhdha al-Khuza'i narrated to us, from al-Mu'addal, from Ibn Umar, that Umar had nabidh made for him in a waterskin in which was fifteen, or sixteen, then he came to it and tasted it, and found it sweet, so he said: As though you lessened its dregs."
"6068","6466 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Uqayl narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, that he said: Mu'adh ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Uthman al-Taymi informed me that his father Abd al-Rahman ibn Uthman said: I accompanied Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, to Makkah, and a caravan from Thaqif gifted him two flat vessels of nabidh, and the flat vessel is above the small waterskin, and below the large waterskin. Abd al-Rahman said: So Umar drank one of them, and did not drink the other until what was in it became strong, then Umar went and drank from it, and found it had become strong, so he said: Break it with water."
"6069","6467 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it. So when it is established by what we mentioned from Umar the permissibility of the little of strong nabidh, while he had heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Every intoxicant is unlawful, what he did in this was proof that what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited by his saying that with him of strong nabidh is intoxication from it, nothing else; either he heard that from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as speech, or he saw it as opinion. So whatever is from him in that, should it be he saw it as opinion, his opinion in that with us is proof, especially when his mentioned act in the reports which we narrated from him was in the presence of the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and no denier from among them denied him, so that indicates their following him upon it. And this Abdillah ibn Umar, and he is one of the men who narrated from him from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Every intoxicant is unlawful. And there was narrated from him from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what [follows]."
"6070","6468 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Salam narrated to us, from Layth, from Abd al-Malik, nephew of al-Qa'qa' ibn Shawr, from Ibn Umar, he said: I witnessed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) brought a drink, and he brought it near to his mouth, frowned, and returned it, when a man said: O Messenger of Allah, is it unlawful? So he returned the drink, then returned with water and poured it upon it, mentioning twice or thrice, then said: When these skins ferment upon you, break their strength with water."
"6071","6469 - Wuhban ibn Uthman al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Abu Hammam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to me, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, he said: Qurrah al-Ijli narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Malik ibn Akhi al-Qa'qa' narrated to me, from Ibn Umar, the like of it."
"6072","6470 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Asbat ibn Muhammad narrated to me, from al-Shaybani, from Abd al-Malik ibn Nafi', he said: I asked Ibn Umar and said: Our family makes nabidh in a skin, such that were I to exert myself with it, it would take effect in me? So Ibn Umar said: Transgression is only upon one who intends transgression; I witnessed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at this corner, and a man brought him a cup of nabidh. Then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Abu Umayyah, except that he said: So break them with water. So in this is the permissibility of the little of strong nabidh. And the most deserving thing for us, when there was narrated from him this from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and there was narrated from him from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Every intoxicant is unlawful, is to place each one of the two sayings upon a meaning other than the meaning upon which is the other saying. So his saying: Every intoxicant is unlawful is upon the amount which intoxicates of it of nabidh, and what is in the other hadith is upon the permissibility of the little of strong nabidh. And there was narrated from Abu Mas'ud al-Ansari, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of this hadith of Ibn Umar."
"6073","6471 - Fahd informed us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn al-Yaman narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Mansur, from Khalid ibn Sa'd, from Abu Mas'ud, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) thirsted around the Ka'bah, and asked for drink, so he was brought nabidh from the nabidh of the watering place, so he smelled it and frowned, then poured upon it from the water of Zamzam, then drank. So a man said: Is it unlawful? So he said: No. And there was narrated in that from Abu Musa al-Ash'ari, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what [follows]."
"6074","6472 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Burdah ibn Abi Musa, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me, me and Mu'adh, to Yemen, so we said: O Messenger of Allah, therein are two drinks made from wheat and barley, one of them called mizr, and the other called bit', so what do we drink? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Drink, but do not become intoxicated."
"6075","6473 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Raja narrated to us, he said: Isra'il informed me, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Burdah, from his father, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me, me and Mu'adh, to Yemen. So I said: You sent us to a land whose people's drink is abundant, so he said: Drink, but do not drink an intoxicant."
"6076","6474 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: al-Fudayl ibn Marzuq narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it. So when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Abu Musa and Mu'adh, when they asked about bit': Drink but do not become intoxicated, and do not drink an intoxicant, that was proof that the ruling of the amount which intoxicates of that drink is contrary to the ruling of what does not intoxicate of it. So that indicates that what Abu Musa mentioned from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from what we mentioned from him in the first section of his saying: Every intoxicant is unlawful, is only upon the amount which intoxicates, not upon the substance whose much intoxicates. And we narrated the hadith of Abu Salamah, from Aishah, in the answer of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the one who asked him about bit' with his saying: Every drink that intoxicates is unlawful. So if we place that upon the little of the drink whose much intoxicates, it contradicts the answer of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Mu'adh and Abu Musa al-Ash'ari. And if we place it upon the prohibition of intoxication specifically, not upon the prohibition of the drink, it agrees with the hadith of Abu Musa. And the most deserving thing for us is carrying the reports upon the aspect which does not contradict when carried upon it. And there was also narrated from Abdillah ibn Mas'ud in that what [follows]."
"6077","6475 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed me, from his father, from Labid ibn Shimas, he said: Abdillah said: The people sit upon drink while it is lawful for them, and they do not cease until it becomes unlawful upon them."
"6078","6476 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, he said: Hammad informed me, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah ibn Qays, that he ate with Abdillah ibn Mas'ud bread and meat, he said: Then we were brought strong nabidh which the wife of Sirin had soaked in a green jar, and they drank from it."
"6079","6477 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to me, he said: Nu'aym and others narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj informed me, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, he said: I asked Ibn Mas'ud about the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding the intoxicant, he said: The last drink of it. So this Abdillah ibn Mas'ud, there was narrated from him regarding the permissibility of the little of strong nabidh, from his act and saying, what we mentioned, and from the interpretation of the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Every intoxicant is unlawful, upon what we described. And there was also narrated from Abdillah ibn Abbas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what indicates this."
"6080","6478 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad al-Zubayri narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ali ibn Badhimah, from Qays ibn Habtar, he said: I asked Ibn Abbas about the green jar and the red jar. So he said: The first who asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that was the delegation of Abd al-Qays, so he said: Do not drink in gourds, nor in varnished vessels, nor in hollowed stumps, and drink in skins. So they said: O Messenger of Allah, what if it becomes strong in skins? He said: Pour water upon it, and he said to them the third or fourth time: Then pour it out."
"6081","6479 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Raja narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, from Ali ibn Badhimah, from Qays ibn Habtar, from Ibn Abbas, that he was asked about the jar, and he mentioned the like of that. So in this hadith is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted them to drink from the nabidh of skins, even if it became strong. So if one says: In his commanding them to pour it out is counted proof of abrogation of what preceded of permissibility? It is said to them: How can that be so? While there was narrated from Ibn Abbas, from his speech after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Wine was prohibited by itself, and intoxication from every drink. And we mentioned that with its chain in what preceded of this book, and it is he who narrated from him what I mentioned. So that indicates that the prohibition in drinks was upon wine by itself, its little and much, and intoxication from other than it. And how is it permissible upon Ibn Abbas, with his knowledge and excellence, that he narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what necessitates the prohibition of strong nabidh, then says: Wine was prohibited by itself, and intoxication from every drink? Teaching the people that the little of drink from other than wine, even if its much intoxicates, is lawful? This is not permissible upon him with us. But the meaning of what he intended by the pouring out of nabidh in the hadith of Qays is that he did not trust them upon it lest they hasten in drinking it and become intoxicated, while intoxication is prohibited upon them, so he commanded them to pour it out for that. And there was also narrated in the like of this what [follows]."
"6082","6480 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn al-Haytham ibn al-Jahm al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Awf ibn Abi Jamilah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Qamus Zayd ibn Ali narrated to me, from one of the delegation who came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in the delegation of Abd al-Qays, or it be Qays ibn al-Nu'man, for I have forgotten his name, that they asked him about drinks, so he said: Do not drink in gourds, nor in hollowed stumps, and drink in the lawful skin tied over it; so if it becomes strong of it, break it with water, and if it overcomes you, pour it out. So if one says: You narrated in this chapter from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, what you mentioned in the hadith of Amr ibn Maymun and others, while there was narrated from him contrary to that. So he mentioned what [follows]."
"6083","6481 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb informed me, from al-Zuhri, he said: al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid narrated to me, that Umar ibn al-Khattab went out, prayed upon a funeral, then turned to the people and said to them: I just now found from Ubaydullah ibn Umar the smell of drink, so I asked him about it, and he claimed that it is tila, and I shall ask about it; if it intoxicates I shall flog him. He said: Then I witnessed Umar after that flogging Ubaydullah eighty for the smell of the drink which was found from him."
"6084","6482 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me, that Malik informed him, from Ibn Shihab, from al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid, that Umar ibn al-Khattab went out upon them and said: I found from so-and-so the smell of drink, and he claimed that it is the drink of tila; I shall ask about what he drank; if it intoxicates I shall flog him. So Umar flogged him the full hadd. He said: So this Umar had applied hadd in the drink which intoxicates, so this contradicts what you narrated from Amr ibn Maymun and others from him. It is said to him: This does not contradict that, because Umar said in this hadith: And I shall ask about what he drank, what was intoxicating I shall flog him. So it bears that he meant by that: The amount which he drank, i.e.: If that amount intoxicates, then I knew that he had become intoxicated, and the hadd became due upon him. And this is the most deserving upon which the interpretation of this hadith is carried, so that it does not contradict the rest of the hadiths which I narrated from him. And there was also narrated from Abu Hurayrah in this what [follows]."
"6085","6483 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn Aslam narrated to me, from Sumayy, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you enters upon his Muslim brother and he feeds him food, let him eat from his food, and not ask about it; and if he gives him drink, let him drink from it, and not ask about it; but if he fears from it, let him break it with something. So in this hadith is the permissibility of drinking nabidh. So if one says: He only permitted it after its breaking with water, and the departure of its strength. It is said to him: This is corrupt speech, because had it been in its state of strength unlawful, it would not become lawful, even if its strength departed by pouring water upon it. Do you not see that had wine had water poured in it, until the water overcame it, that that is unlawful. So when in this hadith the strong drink was permitted when broken with water, it is established by that that before it is broken with water it is not unlawful. So it is established by what we narrated in this chapter the permissibility of what does not intoxicate of strong nabidh, and it is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them, Exalted."
"6086","3 - Chapter: Soaking in gourds, hantam, hollowed stumps, and varnished vessels. 6484 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Qawariri narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Sufyan al-Thawri, from Sulayman, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from al-Harith ibn Suwayd, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade gourds and varnished vessels."
"6087","6485 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Hisham al-Dastuwai narrated to us, he said: Ayyub narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he said: Ibn Umar was asked about the nabidh of the jar, so he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited it. So I came to Ibn Abbas and mentioned that to him, so he said: He spoke truth. I said: Which jar? He said: Everything from earthenware."
"6088","6486 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Ayyub, from a man, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, the like of it."
"6089","6487 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad al-Zubayri narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ali ibn Badhimah, he said: Qays ibn Habtar narrated to me, he said: I asked Ibn Abbas about the green jar and the red. So he said: The first who asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was the delegation of Abd al-Qays, so they said: We obtain from dates, so he said: Do not drink in gourds, nor in varnished vessels, nor in hollowed stumps, nor in jars."
"6090","6488 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Yahya al-Bahrani, he said: I heard Ibn Abbas saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade gourds, hantam, hollowed stumps, and varnished vessels."
"6091","6489 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah and Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Abu Hamzah, he said: I heard Ibn 'Abbas saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the delegation of 'Abd al-Qays from dubba' (gourds), hantam, and naqir. In Shu'bah's hadith: (and perhaps he said: naqir and muzaffat) in both of their hadiths together. And in Shu'bah's hadith: So memorize them from me, and inform those behind you of them."
"6092","6490 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd and Abu Hilal narrated to us, from Abu Jamrah, from Ibn 'Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the delegation of 'Abd al-Qays from hantam, naqir, and muzaffat; and in Hammad's hadith: (and dubba')."
"6093","6491 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Ya'la ibn Hakim, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he said: I heard Ibn 'Umar saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade nabidh of the jar. He said: So I went to Ibn 'Abbas and said: Do you not hear what Ibn 'Umar is saying? He said: And what is he saying? I said: He is saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade nabidh of the jar. He said: Ibn 'Umar spoke the truth; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade nabidh of the jar."
"6094","6492 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Amir al-'Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, he said: I heard Abu al-Hakam saying: I asked Ibn 'Abbas about nabidh, and he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade nabidh of the jar, dubba', and muzaffat. He said: And I asked Ibn al-Zubayr and he said the same. He said: And I asked Ibn 'Umar and he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade nabidh of the jar, dubba', and muzaffat. He said: And my brother informed me, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"6095","6493 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Amir al-'Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Aqil, from 'Ata' ibn Yasar, from Maymunah, and from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from 'A'ishah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Do not make nabidh in dubba', muzaffat, naqir, and jars."
"6096","6494 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Samad narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, he said: I asked 'A'ishah about what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade among the vessels in which nabidh is made, and she said: The pitched vessel (muzaffat)."
"6097","6495 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn 'Ubadah narrated to us, from Hammad, from Shu'bah, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, he said: I asked 'A'ishah about the vessels which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade. She said: The gourd, and the pitched vessel; and they are green jars that used to be brought from Egypt, pitched."
"6098","6496 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Mansur, he said: I heard Ibrahim narrating from al-Aswad, he said: I asked 'A'ishah about what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade among the vessels in which nabidh is made, and she said: The pitched vessel."
"6099","6497 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Samad narrated to us, from Shu'bah, he said: I heard Mansur, and he mentioned with his chain the same. He said: I said: And the jars? She said: I will not add for you beyond what you have heard."
"6100","6498 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Shayban Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from al-Ash'ath ibn Abi al-Sha'tha', he said: 'Abdullah ibn Ma'qil al-Muharibi narrated to me, he said: I heard 'A'ishah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade making nabidh in hantam, dubba', and muzaffat."
"6101","6499 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, he said: Qatadah narrated to me, he said: Four men narrated to me, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, and five women narrated to me, from 'A'ishah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade nabidh of the jar."
"6102","6500 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Imran, or 'Imran ibn 'Ubaydullah, narrated to us, he said: I heard 'Abdullah ibn Shimas saying: I asked 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade hantamah, and it is the jar, and dubba', muzaffat, and naqir."
"6103","6501 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: al-Ash'ath narrated to us, he said: I heard Habbah al-'Urani saying: I heard 'A'ishah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade dubba', hantam, naqir, and muzaffat."
"6104","6502 - 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Thabit, he said: I said to Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both: Did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbid nabidh of the jar? He said: They have claimed that."
"6105","6503 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hudbah ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah informed us, from Thabit, he said: I said to Ibn 'Umar: Did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbid nabidh of the jar? He said: They have claimed that."
"6106","6504 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed in one of his expeditions, then left before I reached him, so I asked: What did he say? They said: He forbade making nabidh in dubba' and muzaffat."
"6107","6505 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Tawus, from Ibn 'Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade nabidh of the jar."
"6108","6506 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from 'Ubaydullah, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the gourd and muzaffat."
"6109","6507 - 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Abu Khaythamah narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir and Ibn 'Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade naqir, dubba' and muzaffat."
"6110","6508 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us. (Ha') 6509 - And Ibn Marzuq also narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn 'Umar narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from 'Uqbah, and he is Ibn Hurayth, from Ibn 'Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the jar, dubba', and muzaffat, and commanded that nabidh be made in waterskins."
"6111","6510 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Muharib ibn Dithar, from Ibn 'Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade dubba', hantam, and muzaffat; he said: I do not know, and did he mention naqir or not?"
"6112","6511 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn 'Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn Murrah narrated to me, from Zadham, he said: I said to Ibn 'Umar: Inform me of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade among the vessels, and explain it to us in our language. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade hantam, and it is what you call the jar; and he forbade dubba', and it is what you call the gourd; and he forbade muzaffat, and it is the pitched one; and he forbade naqir, and it is the palm trunk hollowed out and pierced; and he commanded that nabidh be made in waterskins."
"6113","6512 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, from Hammad, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade dubba', muzaffat, and naqir."
"6114","6513 - 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr said: I heard Jabir ibn 'Abdullah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the pitched jar, dubba', and naqir."
"6115","6514 - 'Ali narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu Qaza'ah informed me, that Abu Nadrah and Hasan informed him, that Abu Sa'id al-Khudri informed them, that when the delegation of 'Abd al-Qays came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), they said: O Prophet of Allah, may Allah make us ransom for you, what is proper for us of drinks? He said: Do not drink in naqir. They said: O Prophet of Allah, may Allah make us ransom for you, we do not know what naqir is? He said: Yes, the trunk hollowed in its middle; nor in dubba', nor in hantamah."
"6116","6515 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Ayyash al-Raqqam narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding what is made in pitched vessels and in dubba', and he said: Every intoxicant is forbidden."
"6117","6516 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Taymi narrating from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Sa'id, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade nabidh of the jar."
"6118","6517 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abu Zayd al-Nahwi narrated to us, from Sulayman al-Taymi, and he mentioned with his chain the same."
"6119","6518 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn 'Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Anas ibn Malik, that he informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade dubba' and muzaffat being used for nabidh."
"6120","6519 - 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn al-Ja'd narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah informed us, he said: Sulayman al-Shaybani informed me, he said: I heard 'Abdullah ibn Abi Awfa saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade nabidh of the green jar. He said: I said: And the white? He said: I do not know."
"6121","6520 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb and Sa'id ibn 'Amir narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Sulayman al-Shaybani, from Ibn Abi Awfa, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"6122","6521 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Shimmar al-Duba'i, he said: I heard 'A'idh ibn 'Amr saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade dubba', naqir, muzaffat, and hanatim."
"6123","6522 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Abu al-Tayyah, from Hafs al-Laythi, from 'Imran ibn Husayn, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade hantam."
"6124","6523 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun saying: Hisham ibn Hassan informed us, from Muhammad, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the delegation of 'Abd al-Qays from dubba', hantam, naqir, muzaffat, and the tied leather bag. And he said: Make nabidh in your waterskin, and drink it sweet and good. A man said to him: Do you permit me in something like this? And he gestured with his hands, parting them. He said: Then you will make it like this. And he gestured with his hands wider than that."
"6125","6524 - 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Surayj ibn al-Nu'man al-Jawhari narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri; Abu Salamah informed him that he heard Abu Hurayrah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not make nabidh in dubba', nor in muzaffat. Then Abu Hurayrah says: Avoid hanatim and naqir."
"6126","6525 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn Abi Salamah narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Awza'i saying: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to me, he said: Abu Salamah narrated to me, he said: Abu Hurayrah narrated to me, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade nabidh of the pitched jars, the pitched dubba', and vessels."
"6127","6526 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Nufayli narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Mujahid informed me, he said: I heard Abu Hurayrah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us making nabidh in dubba' and muzaffat."
"6128","6527 - Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Maymun narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from Yahya, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade jars, dubba', and pitched vessels."
"6129","6528 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik informed him, from al-'Ala' ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade making nabidh in dubba' and muzaffat."
"6130","6529 - 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Shababah ibn Sawwar narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Bukayr ibn 'Ata', from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ya'mar al-Dili, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"6131","6530 - 'Ali narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Wiqa' ibn Iyas, from 'Ali ibn Rabi'ah, from Samurah ibn Jundub, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade dubba', hantam, and muzaffat."
"6132","6531 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Samad ibn 'Abd al-Warith narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi 'Amr, from 'Abdullah ibn al-Daylami, from his father, he said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when the prohibition of wine came down, and I said: O Messenger of Allah, we are people of vineyards, and the prohibition of wine has come down, so what shall we do with them? He said: You will make them raisins. He said: O Messenger of Allah, what shall we do with the raisins? He said: You will prepare them with your morning meal, and drink them with your evening meal, and prepare them with your evening meal and drink them with your morning meal. They said: O Messenger of Allah, shall we not delay it until it becomes strong? He said: Do not put it in large jars and dubba'. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to the view that making nabidh in dubba', naqir, hantam, and muzaffat is forbidden, and they argued with these reports. Others opposed them in that, and permitted making nabidh in all vessels, and among the proof for them in that was that these reports which we narrated are all abrogated. And among what was narrated of their abrogation is what:"
"6133","6532 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Ma'mar 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr ibn Abi al-Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Warith narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Yazid narrated to me, he said: al-Nabighah ibn Mukhariq ibn Sulaym narrated to me, he said: My father narrated to me, from 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I had forbidden you vessels, so drink in whatever seems proper to you, but beware of every intoxicant."
"6134","6533 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from 'Ali ibn Zayd, from Rabi'ah ibn Nabighah, from his father, from 'Ali, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"6135","6534 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the same."
"6136","6535 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, from Ayyub ibn Hani', from Masruq ibn al-Ajda', from Ibn Mas'ud, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same, and he added: Indeed, no vessel forbids anything."
"6137","6536 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun saying: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Farqad al-Sabakhi narrated to us, he said: Jabir ibn Zayd narrated to us, that he heard Masruq narrating from 'Abdullah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same as the hadith of 'Ali from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6138","6537 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Sabbah al-Dulabi narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Fayyad, from Abu 'Iyad, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about vessels, and he said: Do not make nabidh in dubba', hantam, and naqir. A Bedouin said: O Messenger of Allah, no vessels? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Drink what is lawful for you, and avoid every intoxicant."
"6139","6538 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya al-Qattan narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Mansur, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade vessels, the Ansar said: We cannot do without them, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Then no, (do so)."
"6140","6539 - Isma'il ibn Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Nafi' ibn Yazid informed us, he said: Abu Hazrah Ya'qub ibn Mujahid narrated to me, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Jabir ibn 'Abdullah informed me, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I had forbidden you to make nabidh in dubba', hantam, and muzaffat, so make nabidh, but I do not permit an intoxicant."
"6141","6540 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd narrated to me that Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban informed him that al-Wasi' ibn Habban narrated to him, that Abu Sa'id al-Khudri narrated to him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) similar to it."
"6142","6541 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Ma'bad and Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, they said: Abu al-Ahwas Sallam ibn Sulaym al-Hanafi narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from al-Qasim ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, from his father, from Abu Burdah ibn Niyar al-Ansari, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I had forbidden you drinking in vessels, so drink in whatever seems proper to you, but do not become intoxicated."
"6143","6542 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Asim al-Nabil narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from 'Alqamah ibn Marthad, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) similar to it."
"6144","6543 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Zubayd, from Muharib ibn Dithar, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"6145","6544b - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, (Ha') and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Yunus narrated to us, they said: Mu'arrif ibn Wasil narrated to us; Muharib ibn Dithar narrated to me, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"6146","6545 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Zubayd al-Yami, from Muharib ibn Dithar, from Ibn Buraydah, from Zuhayr, I think from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) similar to it."
"6147","6546 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, from Abu Ja'far, from al-Rabi' ibn Anas, from Abu al-'Aliyah and others, from 'Abdullah ibn al-Mughaffal, he said: I witnessed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he forbade nabidh of the jar, and I witnessed him when he commanded drinking it, and he said: Avoid intoxication."
"6148","6547 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, he said: Khalid al-Hadhdha' informed us, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: When the delegation of 'Abd al-Qays returned, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every man is accountable for himself; let every people make nabidh in whatever seems proper to them. So by these reports the abrogation of what preceded them is established, of what we narrated in this chapter on the prohibition of making nabidh in the mentioned vessels. And the permissibility of making nabidh in all vessels is established, and this is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them. And among what also indicates that:"
"6149","6548 - That Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Abu Ja'far narrated to us, from al-Rabi', he said: I entered upon Anas, and I saw his nabidh in a green jar."
"6150","6549 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Hammad ibn Abi Sulayman, he said: I entered upon Anas ibn Malik at Wasit al-Qasab, and I saw his nabidh in a green jar, made for him in it. So this is Anas ibn Malik making nabidh in vessels, and he is one of those who narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the prohibition of making nabidh in them, so it indicates the establishment of its abrogation."
"6151","26 - Book of Dislike. 1 - Chapter on trimming the mustache. 6550 - Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj al-Hadrami narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us. (Ha') 6551 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Affan narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from 'Ali ibn Zayd, from Salamah ibn Muhammad, from 'Ammar ibn Yasir, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The fitrah is ten; then he mentioned trimming the mustache."
"6152","6552 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Waki' narrated to us, from Zakariyya, from Mus'ab ibn Shaybah, from Talq ibn Habib, from 'Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from 'A'ishah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"6153","6553 - 'Abd al-Ghani ibn Rifa'ah ibn Abi 'Aqil and Yunus narrated to us, they said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: The fitrah is five; then he mentioned the same."
"6154","6554 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: al-Mas'udi narrated to us, from Abu 'Awn al-Thaqafi, from al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw a man with a long mustache, so he called for a siwak and a blade, and trimmed the man's mustache upon the siwak stick."
"6155","6555 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Raja' narrated to us, he said: al-Mas'udi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn 'Ubaydullah narrated to us, from al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah, that a man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a long mustache, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called for a siwak, then called for a blade, and trimmed the man's mustache upon a siwak."
"6156","6556 - Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Abi al-Wazir narrated to us. (Ha') 6557 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mis'ar, from Abu Sakhrah Jami' ibn Shaddad al-Muharibi, from al-Mughirah ibn 'Abdullah, from al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took from my mustache upon a siwak. Abu Ja'far said: So a group of the people of Madinah went to these reports, and chose for them trimming the mustache over shaving it off. Others opposed them in that and said: Rather shaving the mustaches closely is recommended; we see it better than trimming them. And they argued in that with what:"
"6157","6558 - Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Muhriz narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Salih narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to cut his mustache, and Ibrahim (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to cut his mustache."
"6158","6559 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to me, from Abu Bakr ibn Nafi', from his father. (Ha') 6560 - And Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, both from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Shave the mustaches closely, and leave the beards."
"6159","6561 - Ibn Abi 'Aqil narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to me, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"6160","6562 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, he said: Abu Ja'far al-Madini narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn 'Abdullah ibn Abi Talhah narrated to us, from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he added: And do not resemble the Jews."
"6161","6563 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from al-'Ala' ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Cut the mustaches, and lengthen, or leave, the beards."
"6162","6564 - Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from 'Umar ibn Abi Salamah, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Shave the mustaches closely, and leave the beards. So this is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he commanded shaving the mustaches closely, so closeness is established by that upon what we mentioned, in the hadith of Ibn 'Umar. And in the hadith of Ibn 'Abbas and Abu Hurayrah: Cut the mustaches; so that may be cutting accompanied by closeness, and may be upon less than that. So the opposition of the hadith of Ibn 'Umar is established, with the hadith of Abu Hurayrah, 'Ammar, and 'A'ishah, which we mentioned at the beginning of this chapter. As for the hadith of al-Mughirah, there is no proof in it for anything, for it is possible the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did that, and there were no scissors present with which he could shave the mustache closely. The hadith of 'Ammar and 'A'ishah and Abu Hurayrah also admits another meaning in that: it is possible the fitrah is what is indispensable, and it is trimming the mustache, and what is beyond that is additional merit. So all the reports we narrated in this chapter are established, with no contradiction, and by their establishment it is obligatory that shaving closely is better than trimming. And this is the meaning of this chapter, by way of reports. As for by way of reasoning, we saw shaving is commanded in ihram, and shortening is permitted. So shaving was better than shortening, and shortening, whoever wished did it, and whoever wished added upon it, except that by his addition upon it he is greater in reward than one who shortened. Reasoning upon that is that likewise is the ruling of the mustache: trimming it is good, and shaving it closely is better and more excellent. And this is the school of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them. And there has been narrated from a group of the predecessors what:"
"6163","6565 - Ibn Abi 'Aqil narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash informed me, he said: Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid narrated to me, he said: I saw Anas ibn Malik and Wathilah ibn al-Asqa' shaving their mustaches closely and leaving their beards, and dyeing them yellow. 6566 - Isma'il said: And 'Uthman ibn 'Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi' al-Madani narrated to me, he said: I saw 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar, Abu Hurayrah, Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, Abu Usayd al-Sa'idi, Rafi' ibn Khadij, Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, Anas ibn Malik, and Salamah ibn al-Akwa' doing that."
"6164","6567 - Muhammad ibn al-Nu'man narrated to us, he said: Abu Thabit narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from 'Uthman ibn 'Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi', he said: I saw Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, Abu Usayd, Rafi' ibn Khadij, Sahl ibn Sa'd, 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar, Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, and Abu Hurayrah shaving their mustaches closely."
"6165","6568 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: 'Asim ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from his father, from Ibn 'Umar, that he used to shave his mustache closely, until the whiteness of the skin was seen."
"6166","6569 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hamid ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Hatib, he said: I saw Ibn 'Umar shaving his mustache closely."
"6167","6570 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from 'Uthman ibn Ibrahim al-Hatibi, he said: I saw Ibn 'Umar shaving his mustache closely, as if plucking it."
"6168","6571 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn 'Umar, that he used to shave his mustache closely."
"6169","6572 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, from Ibn Lahi'ah, from 'Uqbah ibn Muslim, he said: I never saw anyone more thorough in shaving his mustache than Ibn 'Umar; he used to shave it closely, until the skin was seen. So these Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to shave their mustaches closely, and among them is Abu Hurayrah, and he is among those from whom we narrated, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Among the fitrah is trimming the mustache. So that indicates that trimming the mustache is of the fitrah, and it is of what is indispensable, and that what is beyond that of closeness is better, and in it is attainment of good that is not in trimming."
"6170","2 - Chapter on facing the qiblah with the private parts for defecation and urination. 6573 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from 'Ata' ibn Yazid al-Laythi; he heard Abu Ayyub al-Ansari saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not face the qiblah for defecation nor for urination, but face east or west. So we came to Syria, and found privies built facing the qiblah, so we turn away from them, and seek Allah's forgiveness."
"6171","6574 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, and he mentioned with his chain the same, except that he did not mention the saying of Abu Ayyub: So we came to Syria, to the end of the hadith."
"6172","6575 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Abu Mus'ab narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jariyah, that Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, then he mentioned the same, and he also mentioned the words of Abu Ayyub."
"6173","6576 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, that Malik narrated to him, from Ishaq ibn 'Abdullah ibn Abi Talhah, from Rafi' ibn Ishaq, a freedman of the family of al-Shifa', a woman, and he was called the freedman of Abu Talhah, that he heard Abu Ayyub al-Ansari saying, while he was in Egypt: By Allah, I do not know what to do with these lavatories, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you goes for defecation or urination, let him not face the qiblah, nor turn his back to it with his private part."
"6174","6577 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, that Malik narrated to him, from Nafi', that a man of the Ansar informed him, from his father, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding facing the qiblah for defecation or urination."
"6175","6578 - Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: 'Abidah ibn Humayd al-Nahwi narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from a man of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); a man said to him: I think your companion teaches you, until he even teaches you how you go to relieve yourselves. He said to him: Yes, even if you mock; that he does so, that he forbids us when one of us goes to relieve himself, to face the qiblah."
"6176","6579 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: 'Amr ibn al-Harith, al-Layth, and Ibn Lahi'ah informed me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from 'Abdullah ibn al-Harith ibn Jaz' al-Zubaydi, he said: I am the first who heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Let none of you urinate facing the qiblah, and I am the first who conveyed that to the people."
"6177","6580 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from 'Abdullah ibn al-Harith ibn Jaz', he said: I am the first who heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding the people to urinate facing the qiblah, so I went out to the people and informed them."
"6178","6581 - Abu al-Bishr 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Jarud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Habib informed me, from Jabalah ibn Rafi', he said: I heard 'Abdullah ibn al-Harith al-Zubaydi, and he mentioned similar to it."
"6179","6582 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Sahl ibn Tha'labah narrated to me, from 'Abdullah ibn al-Harith ibn Jaz' al-Zubaydi, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade a man to urinate facing the qiblah, and I am the first who heard that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6180","6583 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Jandal ibn Waliq narrated to us, he said: Hafs narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from Salman, he said: We were forbidden to face the qiblah for relieving ourselves."
"6181","6584 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Ibn 'Ajlan narrated to us, from al-Qa'qa' ibn Hakim, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: I am to you only like a father; I teach you. So when one of you goes to relieve himself, let him not face the qiblah, nor turn his back to it."
"6182","6585 - Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Safwan ibn 'Isa narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn 'Ajlan narrated to us, and he mentioned with a chain the same."
"6183","6586 - Rawh narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Kathir ibn 'Ufayr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Abu al-Aswad, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: When one of you goes out for defecation or urination, let him not face the qiblah, nor turn his back to it, nor face the wind."
"6184","6587 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn Yahya narrated to us, from Abu Zayd, from Ma'qil ibn Abi Ma'qil al-Asadi, and he had accompanied the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us to face the qiblah for defecation or urination."
"6185","6588 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Dawud al-'Attar narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Abu Zayd, the freedman of Banu Tha'labah, narrated to us, from Ma'qil ibn Abi Ma'qil, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"6186","6589 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: Abu Kamil narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn al-Mukhtar narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn Yahya narrated to us, from Abu Zayd, from Ma'qil, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same. So a group went to the dislike of facing the qiblah for defecation or urination in all places, and they argued in that with these reports. And among those who went to that were Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them. Others opposed them in that and said: There is no harm in facing the qiblah for defecation and urination in enclosed places."
"6187","And they argued in that with what: 6590 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, that Malik narrated to him, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from his uncle Wasi' ibn Habban, from Ibn 'Umar, that he used to say: People say: When you sit for your need, do not face the qiblah nor Bayt al-Maqdis. 'Abdullah said: I climbed upon the back of a house, and I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon two bricks, facing Bayt al-Maqdis for his need."
"6188","6591 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Anas narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, and he mentioned with his chain the same."
"6189","6592 - Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym informed us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from his uncle Wasi' ibn Habban, he said: I heard Ibn 'Umar saying: I appeared upon a roof of mine in the house of Hafsah, at an hour I did not think anyone would go out in, and he mentioned the same."
"6190","6593 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Umayyah, Yahya ibn Sa'id, and 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Umar, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from his uncle Wasi' ibn Habban, from Ibn 'Umar, he said: I climbed above the house of Hafsah, and behold the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sitting upon his seat, facing the qiblah, turning his back to Syria."
"6191","6594 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn 'Ajlan narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Yahya, from Wasi' ibn Habban, from Ibn 'Umar, that he said: People narrate from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about defecation a hadith, and I once looked, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was upon the back of a house, relieving his need, screened by bricks, and I saw him facing the qiblah."
"6192","6595 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha', from Khalid ibn Abi al-Salt, he said: We were with 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz, and they mentioned facing the qiblah with the private part. 'Irak ibn Malik said: 'A'ishah said: It was mentioned before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that people dislike facing the qiblah with the private parts. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Have they done that? Turn my seat toward the qiblah."
"6193","6596 - Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, from Abu Qatadah, that he saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) urinating facing the qiblah."
"6194","6597 - 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, he said: Aban ibn Salih narrated to us, from Mujahid ibn Jabr, from Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had forbidden us to face the qiblah and turn our backs to it with our private parts for urination, then I saw him a year before his death, urinating facing the qiblah."
"6195","6598 - 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha', from Khalid ibn Abi al-Salt, he said: We were with 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz, and they mentioned a man sitting in the privy facing the qiblah, and they disliked that, so 'Irak ibn Malik narrated, from 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from 'A'ishah, that that was mentioned before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Have they done that? Turn my seat toward the qiblah. So these reports were a proof for the people of this view against the people of the first view, and necessitating the proof against them; for in these reports is the delay of permission after prohibition, upon what we mentioned in the hadith of Jabir, so they abrogate the reports we mentioned at the beginning of this chapter. And a group differed with both views together, and said: Rather we say: None of these reports abrogates any of them. That is because 'Abdullah ibn al-Harith informed in his hadith that he was the first who heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding that. He said: And I am the first who conveyed that to the people. So it is possible that prohibition did not fall upon urination and defecation in all places, and fell upon a specific part of them, namely the open deserts. Then Abu Ayyub came, and his narration from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was the prohibition specifically, so that admits what the hadith of Ibn Jaz' admitted upon what we explained, and the dislike of facing in the mentioned lavatories is from his opinion, and he did not narrate it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So facing may be permissible until he heard from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what he heard, and learned that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) meant by it the deserts, then he himself judged for houses by his opinion with the same. And it is possible the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) meant houses and deserts, except that there is no proof in that from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) clarifying to us that he meant one of the two meanings to the exclusion of the other. And the hadith of 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid from Salman, and the hadith of Ma'qil ibn Abi Ma'qil, and the hadith of Abu Hurayrah, of what is in them from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), are likewise also. Then we returned to what we narrated of permission, and behold Ibn 'Umar says: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon the back of a house facing the qiblah. So it may be upon his permission to turn the back to the qiblah for defecation or urination in deserts and houses. And it may be upon permission for that in houses specifically, so he meant by what was narrated from him of prohibition upon deserts specifically. So it is more proper for us to make this hadith additional to the first hadiths, not contradicting them, so this is upon houses, and those first hadiths are upon deserts, and this is the view of Malik ibn Anas."
"6196","6599 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us that he heard Malik saying that. Then we returned to the hadith of Abu Qatadah, and in it: That he saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) urinating facing the qiblah. So he may have seen him where Ibn 'Umar saw him, so the meaning of his hadith and the hadith of Ibn 'Umar is the same. Or he may have seen him in a desert, so it contradicts the hadith of Ibn 'Umar, and abrogates the first hadiths, but with us it does not abrogate them, until it is known with certainty that it abrogated them. As for the hadith of Jabir, in it is the prohibition from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of facing the qiblah and turning the back to it, for defecation or urination, and he did not clarify a place. So it may also be upon what we explained and clarified from the hadith of Abu Ayyub, so there is no proof in it either necessitating opposition to the hadith of Ibn 'Umar and Abu Qatadah. Jabir said in his hadith: Then I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) urinating facing the qiblah. So it may be that urination was in the place upon which the first prohibition of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not fall, so we do not know anything of these reports abrogating anything of them. Then we returned to the hadith of 'Irak, and in it that it was mentioned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that people dislike facing the qiblah with their private parts. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Turn my seat facing the qiblah. So it is possible he disapproved their saying, because they disliked that in all places, so he commanded turning his seat toward the qiblah, to rebut them, and to inform that his prohibition did not fall upon that, but the prohibition fell upon facing it in one place to the exclusion of another. And it is possible he meant by that the abrogation of the first prohibition in all places, for the prohibition had fallen in the first reports against that, so there is no proof in it either for abrogation or otherwise. So when the ruling of these reports was such, it was more proper for us to validate them all. So we make what is in them of prohibition upon the deserts, and what is in them of permission upon houses, so that none of them contradicts another."
"6197","6600 - And: Ibn Abi 'Imran narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Isma'il narrated to us. (Ha'): And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, from Hatim, from 'Isa ibn Abi 'Isa al-Khayyat. (Ha') 6601 - And Isma'il narrated to us, he said: 'Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: 'Isa narrated to us, from al-Sha'bi, that he asked him about the disagreement of these two hadiths, and al-Sha'bi said: By Allah, they both spoke the truth. As for the hadith of Abu Hurayrah, it is upon the deserts; indeed Allah and His angels pray, so do not face them; and these privies of yours have no qiblah in them. Upon this meaning these reports are carried, so that none of them contradicts another."
"6198","3 - Chapter on eating garlic, onion, and leek. 6602 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Talhah ibn 'Amr informed me, from 'Ata', from Ibn 'Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever eats of these greens of yours, possessors of odor, let him not approach us in our mosques, for the angels are harmed by what the children of Adam are harmed by."
"6199","6603 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Raja' narrated to us, from 'Ubaydullah, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever eats of this tree, let him not come to the mosques."
"6200","6604 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Ibn Numayr narrated to us, from 'Ubaydullah, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever eats of this herb, let him not approach the mosque, until its odor goes away; meaning: garlic."
"6201","6605 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah and Fahd narrated to us, they said: 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Ibn al-Had narrated to me, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade eating garlic at Khaybar."
"6202","6606 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Qays narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Sharik ibn Hanbal, from 'Ali, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever eats of this herb, let him not approach us or harm us in our mosque."
"6203","6607 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Salih al-Hanafi Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, he said: Ma'n ibn 'Isa narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Sa'd, from al-Zuhri, from 'Abbad ibn Tamim, from his uncle, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever eats of this tree, let him not approach our mosques; meaning garlic."
"6204","6608 - Ahmad ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Ma'mar narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Warith narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Suhayb narrated to us, he said: A man asked Anas: What did you hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying about garlic? He said: Meaning I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever eats of this tree, let him not approach us, nor pray with us."
"6205","6609 - Muhammad ibn 'Amr narrated to us, he said: 'Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from 'Ata', from Jabir, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever eats of this herb, let him not approach us in our mosque, or let him not approach our mosque."
"6206","6610 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Qays ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us, from Bishr ibn Bashir, from his father, and he was of the companions of the Tree, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever eats of this tree, let him not hold intimate conversation with us."
"6207","6611 - 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Hakam ibn 'Atiyyah narrated to us, from Abu al-Rabab, from Ma'qil ibn Yasar, he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey of his, and we camped in a place in which were garlic plants, and his companions spread among them and ate of them, then went in the morning to the prayer place. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) found the odor of garlic, and said: Do not approach this tree, then come to the mosques. He said: Then they came a second time to the prayer place, and he found its odor, and said: Whoever eats of this tree, let him not approach the prayer place."
"6208","6612 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Qays narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Sharik ibn Hanbal, from 'Ali, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever eats this herb, let him not approach us, or harm us in our mosques. Abu Ja'far said: So a group disliked eating odoriferous herbs altogether, and they argued in that with these reports. Others opposed them in that, and said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade eating them, not because they are forbidden, but so that none who attends the mosque with him be harmed by their odor, and there have come in that other reports which indicate that."
"6209","6613 - 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Wahhab ibn 'Ata' narrated to us, he said: Sa'id narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Ma'dan ibn Abi Talhah al-Ya'mari, that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: O people, you eat of two foul trees, this garlic and this onion, and I would see a man in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) whose odor was found, and he would be taken by his hand and brought out to al-Baqi'; so whoever ate them, let him kill them by cooking. So this is 'Umar informing of what they used to do with one who ate them in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he permitted eating them after they are killed by cooking. So that indicates the prohibition of them was not for forbiddance. And:"
"6210","6614 - 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Maysarah narrated to us, from Mu'awiyah ibn Qurrah, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever eats of these two foul trees, let him not approach our mosque; and if you must eat them, kill them by cooking. So this is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitting eating them after the departure of their odor. So that indicates his prohibition of eating them was only from dislike of their odor, not that they are forbidden in themselves. And:"
"6211","6615 - 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Abu Hilal al-Rasibi and others narrated to us, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abu Burdah ibn Abi Musa, from al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah, he said: I ate garlic in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then came to the mosque, having missed a rak'ah, and joined them in prayer, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) found its odor. When he gave salam he said: Whoever eats of this foul tree, let him not approach our prayer place, until its odor goes away. So I completed my prayer, and when I gave salam I said: O Messenger of Allah, I adjure you to give me your hand, so he gave me his hand (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I put it in my sleeve, until I reached my chest, and found it bound, and he said: You have an excuse. So in the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): That whoever eats of this foul tree, let him not approach us in our mosque, until its odor goes away, is proof that he only forbade eating it so that its odor not harm one who attends the mosque, not that eating it is forbidden."
"6212","6616 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn 'Amir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Jabir ibn Samurah: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ate of food, he would send its remainder to Abu Ayyub. He said: So one day he sent to him a bowl from which he had not eaten, and Abu Ayyub came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, is it forbidden? He said: No, but I disliked it for its odor. He said: Then I dislike what you disliked."
"6213","6617 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Ubaydullah ibn Abi Yazid, from his father, he said: Umm Ayyub al-Ansariyyah, with whom the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had stayed, stayed with me, and she narrated to me that they prepared for him food, in which were some of these herbs, and they brought it to him, and he disliked it, and said to his companions: Eat it, for I am not like any of you; I fear I may harm my companion."
"6214","6618 - Yunus narrated to us another time, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Ubaydullah, he said: I heard Umm Ayyub al-Ansariyyah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stayed with me, and I brought him food, in which were some of these herbs, and he did not eat it, and said: I dislike harming my companion."
"6215","6619 - And Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu al-Khayr, from Abu Ruhm al-Sama'i, that Abu Ayyub narrated to him, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, you used to send food and I would look, and when I saw the trace of your fingers, I would put my hand in it, until this food which you sent, and I looked in it, and did not see in it the trace of your fingers. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes, in it is onion, so I disliked eating it because of the angel who comes to me; as for you, eat it."
"6216","6620 - Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri' narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, and he mentioned with his chain the same."
"6217","6621 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Ayyash ibn al-Walid al-Raqqam narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-A'la ibn 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Habib narrated to me, from Marthad ibn 'Abdullah, from Abu Umamah, from Abu Ayyub, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same, except that he did not name the tree."
"6218","6622 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Bakr ibn Sawadah, that Sufyan ibn 'Abdullah narrated to him, from Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) similar to it, except that he said: Onion, or leek, and added at its end: And it is not forbidden. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted the people in these reports to eat onion and leek, and that that is not forbidden. If one says: This which you mentioned is only upon what of them was cooked. As for what was uncooked, it is included in the prohibition in the first reports. It is said to him: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in what we mentioned from him in these reports that he only disliked it for its odor, and he permitted his companions to eat it. So whatever retained its odor after cooking was upon its ruling before cooking, since he only disliked eating it in both together, because of its odor. So his permitting them to eat it after cooking while its odor exists indicates that their eating it before cooking is also permitted for them."
"6219","And: 6623 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: 'Ata' ibn Abi Rabah narrated to me, that Jabir ibn 'Abdullah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever eats garlic or onion, let him keep away from us, or keep away from our mosque and sit in his house; and there was brought a pot or vessel in which were greens of herbs, and he found for them an odor, and asked about them and was informed of the herbs in them, and said: Bring them near to one of his companions who was with him, and when he saw him disliking eating it he said: Eat, for I hold intimate conversation with one whom you do not hold conversation with."
"6220","6624 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever eats of leek, let him not come upon us in our mosques, until its odor goes away, for the angels are harmed by what man is harmed by."
"6221","6625 - 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Mu'awiyah al-'Attabi narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us. (Ha') 6626 - And Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Shabbabah ibn Sawwar narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, from Muslim al-A'war, from Habbah, from 'Ali, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us to eat garlic, and said: Were it not that the angel descends upon me, I would have eaten it. So what we mentioned indicates the permissibility of eating them, whether cooked or uncooked, for one who sits in his house, and the dislike of attending the mosque while its odor exists, lest he thereby harm those who attend it of angels and the children of Adam; by this we take, and it is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them."
"6222","4 - Chapter on a man passing by a walled garden: may he eat of it or not? 6627 - 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn 'Asim narrated to us, he said: al-Jurayri narrated to us, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: I think, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: When one of you comes upon a walled garden, let him call its owner three times; if he answers him, otherwise let him eat without spoiling; and when he comes upon sheep, let him call its owner three times; if he answers him, otherwise let him drink without spoiling. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to this and allowed one who passes by a walled garden to call its owner thrice, and if he answers him, otherwise he eats, and likewise for sheep. Others opposed them in that and said: He should not eat without necessity; if there is necessity, eating of that and drinking is permitted for him. They said: And there has been narrated from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri in other than this hadith, what indicates that the permission mentioned in this hadith is upon necessity. 6628 - So they mentioned what Fahd narrated to us, he said: Mukhawwal ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Ismah, he said: I heard Abu Sa'id al-Khudri saying: When the people run out of provisions and take morning milk of camels, let them call the herdsman thrice; if they do not find the herdsman, and find the camels, let them take morning milk of the water-carrying camel's milk, if there is among the camels a water-carrier, and they have no right in the rest of them; if the herdsman comes, let two men hold him and not fight him, and let them drink; if they have dirhams with them, it is forbidden for them except by permission of its people. So in this hadith is proof that what was permitted of that in this first hadith is only upon necessity. And there has come from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in other than this hadith, what also indicates this meaning."
"6223","6629 - Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Bakr ibn Mudar narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Yazid ibn al-Had, from Malik ibn Anas, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Let none of you milk the livestock of his brother without his permission. Would one of you like his storehouse to be approached, his chest broken, and his food carried away? Their udders store for them their foods, so let none of you milk the livestock of a man except by his permission."
"6224","6630 - Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: al-Thawri narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Umayyah, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"6225","6631 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Sabbah narrated to us, he said: Sharik ibn 'Abdullah narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Ism, he said: I heard Abu Sa'id al-Khudri raising it, he said: It is not lawful for anyone to untie the hobble of a she-camel except by permission of its people, for it is their seal upon it."
"6226","6632 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Amir al-'Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from Suhayl, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Sa'id, from Abu Humayd al-Sa'idi, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not lawful for a man to take the staff of his brother without his goodwill; he said: And that is because of the severity of what Allah has forbidden to the Muslims of the property of the Muslim."
"6227","6633 - Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Asbagh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Malik ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Sa'id, from 'Umarah ibn Harithah, from 'Amr ibn Yathribi, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us and said: Nothing of the property of his brother is lawful for a man except with his goodwill. He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, if I meet the sheep of my cousin, may I take of them something? He said: If you meet them carrying a blade and firestick in the wilderness of al-Jamish, do not disturb them. So these reports we mentioned prevent what one who went in interpreting the first hadith to what we mentioned imagined. And had what he went to of that been established, it might be that hadith was in the state of the obligatoriness of hospitality, when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded it, and obligated it for travelers upon those among whom they alight. For:"
"6228","6634 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn 'Umar and Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Mansur, from al-Sha'bi, from al-Miqdam Abu Karimah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The night of the guest is a right obligatory upon every Muslim; if he wakes in his courtyard, it is a debt; if he wishes he demands it, and if he wishes he leaves it."
"6229","6635 - Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the same."
"6230","6636 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Mansur, and he mentioned with his chain the same."
"6231","6637 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrated to us, that Abu Talhah narrated to him, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whatever guest alights with a people, and the guest wakes deprived, he may take to the measure of his hospitality, with no sin upon him."
"6232","6638 - Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: My uncle narrated to us, he said: Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrated to us, from Nu'aym ibn Ziyad, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"6233","6639 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Mushir narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hamzah narrated to us, from al-Zubaydi, from Marwan ibn Ru'bah, that he narrated to him, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi 'Awf al-Jurashi, from al-Miqdam ibn Ma'dikarib, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whatever man stays as guest with a people, and they do not host him, he may recompense them with the like of his hospitality."
"6234","6640 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu al-Khayr, from 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir, he said: We said: O Messenger of Allah, you send us and we pass by people who do not order for us the right of the guest. He said: If you alight with a people and they order for you what befits the guest, accept; and if they do not, take from them the right of the guest which befits. So (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) obligated hospitality in these reports, and made it a debt, and gave one to whom it was due its taking, as he takes a debt. Then that was abrogated. And among what was narrated of its abrogation is what:"
"6235","6641 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, he said: Thabit narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, he said: al-Miqdad ibn al-Aswad narrated to us, he said: I and a companion of mine came, our hearing and sight almost gone from hunger, and we began to present ourselves to people and none hosted us. So we came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, severe hunger struck us, and we presented ourselves to people and none hosted us, so we came to you. So he took us to his house, and with him were four goats, and he said: O Miqdad, milk them, and divide the milk, a portion for every two, and he mentioned a long hadith."
"6236","6642 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Thabit, from Abdur Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from al-Miqdad ibn Amr, he said: I came to Madinah, I and a companion of mine, then he mentioned the like of it. Do you not see that the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not host them as guests, though need had driven them to what is mentioned in this hadith, yet the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not rebuke them for that. So what we have mentioned indicates the abrogation of what had made hospitality obligatory upon people. And we have mentioned earlier in this book of ours, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The property of a Muslim is to a Muslim like the sanctity of his blood."
"6237","And also: 6643 - Rabi narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib, from Abdullah ibn as-Saib, from his father, from his grandfather, that he heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: None of you should take the belongings of his companion, neither in jest nor seriously, and if one of you takes the staff of his brother, let him return it to him. And the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) acted in hospitality according to what..."
"6238","6644 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Aban ibn Yazid al-Attar narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, he said: Abdur Rahman, the freed slave of Sad ibn Abi Waqqas, narrated to us, he said: I was with Sad ibn Abi Waqqas on a journey, and night drove us to the village of a dihqan (chief), and there were camels with their loads upon them. So Sad said to me: If you wish to be a true Muslim, do not eat anything from them, so we spent the night hungry. So this is Sad saying: If it pleases you to be a true Muslim, do not eat anything from them, and that could only be because the reality of his knowledge of it was established with him, since it was with him among the matters of Islam, and he did not demand the villagers for the right of hospitality. So that is proof that hospitality was not obligatory at that time, and Allah, glorified and exalted is He, knows best."
"6239","5 - Chapter on wearing silk. 6645 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to me, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, that some cloaks (aqbiyah) came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and that reached my father Makhramah, so he said: O my son, it has reached me that some cloaks have come to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he is distributing them, so take us to him. He said: So we went, and we found the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his house, so my father said to me: O my son, call the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for me. Al-Miswar said: So I regarded that as grave, and I said: Should I call the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for you? He said: O my son, he is not a tyrant. So I called the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he came out wearing a brocade qaba (long coat) buttoned with gold, and he said: O Makhramah, this I had hidden for you, and he gave it to him. Abu Jafar said: So a group went to this and said: There is no harm in wearing silk, for men and women, and they argued with this hadith for that. Others opposed them in that, and disliked wearing silk for men, and they argued with the successive (mutawatir) reports narrated prohibiting it, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Among them is what..."
"6240","6646 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Muadh ibn Hisham narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Amir ash-Shabi, from Suwayd ibn Ghafalah, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, delivered a sermon at al-Jabiyah and said: The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade wearing silk except for a space of two fingers or three or four."
"6241","6647 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: Muadh narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu Uthman an-Nahdi, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us wearing silk, except for a space of two fingers, or three, or four."
"6242","6648 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Asim al-Ahwal narrated to us, from Abu Uthman an-Nahdi, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Beware of silk, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade it and said: Do not wear of it except what is like this, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gestured with his two fingers."
"6243","6649 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun, and he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"6244","6650 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu Uthman an-Nahdi, he said: The letter of Umar came to us, while I was in Adharbayjan with Utbah ibn Farqad, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us wearing silk except like this, he said: So he informed us that they are the borders (decorative stripes)."
"6245","6651 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, from his father, from Jamil ibn Murrah, from Abu al-Wadi, he said: I saw Ali, and he saw upon a man a cloak shimmering, so he said: Is there silk in it? He said: Yes. So he took it and gathered its two edges between his fingers and tore it, and said: I did not envy you for it, but I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbid silk."
"6246","6652 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Arim narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that Umar said: O Messenger of Allah, I passed by Utarid, or by Labid, while he was offering a silk suit for sale; if only you would buy it for Friday and for delegations. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Silk in this world is worn only by one who has no share in the Hereafter."
"6247","6653 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, that Malik narrated to him, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it, except that he did not mention Utarid nor Labid."
"6248","6654 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus and Amr informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it, and he mentioned that the man was Utarid, or Labid."
"6249","6655 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Mamar narrated to us, he said: Abdul Warith ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Salim ibn Abdullah said to me: What is istabraq? I said: What is thick of dibaj (brocade) and coarse of it. He said: I heard Abdullah ibn Umar saying: Umar ibn al-Khattab saw upon a man a suit of istabraq, so he brought it and said: O Messenger of Allah, buy this and wear it for the delegations of people when they come to you. He said: Silk is worn only by one who has no share. He said: Then time passed for that as it passed. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent to him a suit, and he came to him with it and said: O Messenger of Allah, you sent this to me, though you said about the like of this what you said? He said: I only sent it to you so that you may gain wealth with it. And Abdullah ibn Umar used to dislike the border (alam) on the garment because of this hadith."
"6250","6656 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: I heard as-Saqab ibn Zuhayr narrating from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abdullah ibn Umar, he said: A Bedouin came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wearing a jubbah edged with silk, or he said: buttoned with dibaj, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood up to him angrily and seized the front of his jubbah and pulled him with it, then said: I do not see upon you the clothes of one who has sense, and it is a long hadith, so we have abridged from it this meaning."
"6251","6657 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu Shaykh al-Hunai, he said: I was among a gathering of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with Muawiyah, and he said: I adjure you by Allah, do you know that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade wearing silk? He said: They said: O Allah, yes. He said: And I bear witness."
"6252","6658 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, and he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"6253","6659 - Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, he said: Humayd informed me, from Bakr ibn Abdullah, from Ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Silk is worn only by one who has no share."
"6254","6660 - Muhammad ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hamzah narrated to us, he said: al-Awzai narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to me, he said: Humran narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah performed Hajj, and he called a group of the Ansar in the Kabah and said: I adjure you by Allah, did you not hear that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade garments of silk? They said: O Allah, yes. He said: And I bear witness."
"6255","6661 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Ibn Abi Layla, he said: Hudhayfah asked for drink in al-Madain, so a dihqan brought him a vessel of silver, so he threw it away, then said: I had forbidden him from it but he refused to stop. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade drinking in vessels of gold and silver, and wearing silk and dibaj, and said: Leave it for them in this world, and it is for you in the Hereafter."
"6256","6662 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Ibn Abi Layla, the like of it."
"6257","6663 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Masud ibn Sad al-Jufi narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Abdur Rahman ibn Abi Layla, the like of it."
"6258","6664 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq ad-Darir narrated to us, he said: Ibn Awf narrated to us, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abi Layla, the like of it."
"6259","6665 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Said narrated to us, from his father, from Ali ibn Abdullah, from his father, from Muawiyah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade wearing silk and gold."
"6260","6666 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abu at-Tayyah, from a man of Banu Layth, from Imran ibn Husayn, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade wearing silk."
"6261","6667 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abu at-Tayyah narrated to us, from Hafs al-Laythi, from Imran ibn Husayn, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"6262","6668 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ayyash ar-Raqqam narrated to us, he said: Abdul Ala narrated to us, he said: Said narrated to us, from Matar, from al-Hasan, from Imran ibn al-Husayn, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I do not wear a shirt edged with silk, and al-Hasan pointed to the opening of his shirt."
"6263","6669 - Abdul Ghani ibn Abi Aqil narrated to us, he said: Abdur Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us. And: 6670 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud and Wahb narrated to us, they said: Shubah narrated to us, from al-Ashath ibn Abi ash-Shatha, from Muawiyah ibn Suwayd ibn Muqarrin, from al-Bara ibn Azib, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us wearing silk and dibaj, and drinking in vessels of gold and silver."
"6264","6671 - Muhammad ibn an-Numan narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Thabit al-Bunani, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn az-Zubayr saying: Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever wears silk in this world will not wear it in the Hereafter."
"6265","6672 - Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Abi Abdullah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Dawud as-Sarraj, from Abu Said al-Khudri, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever wears silk in this world will not wear it in the Hereafter, and even if he enters Paradise, the people of Paradise will wear it but he will not wear it."
"6266","6673 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Mamar narrated to us, he said: Abdul Warith narrated to us, he said: Abdul Aziz ibn Suhayb narrated to us, from Anas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever wears silk in this world will not wear it in the Hereafter."
"6267","6674 - Mubashshir ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abdul Aziz ibn Suhayb, and I asked him about silk, so he said: I heard Anas, so I said: From the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Firmly, then he mentioned the like of it."
"6268","6675 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Humayd at-Tawil, from Anas, he said: We used to narrate that."
"6269","6676 - Yunus and Bahr narrated to us; Yunus said: Ibn Wahb informed us, and Bahr said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, that Hisham ibn Abi Ruqayyah al-Lakhmi narrated to him, he said: I heard Maslamah ibn Mukhallad delivering a sermon, saying: Do you not have cotton, flax, to suffice you from wearing silk? And among you is a man who reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); stand, O Uqbah. So Uqbah ibn Amir stood and said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever wears silk in this world will be deprived of wearing it in the Hereafter."
"6270","6677 - Muhammad ibn Humayd ibn Hisham narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hamzah narrated to me, from al-Walid ibn as-Saib, that al-Walid Abu Ammar said: Abu Umamah narrated to us that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Silk in this world is worn only by one who has no share."
"6271","6678 - Husayn ibn Nasr and Muhammad ibn Humayd narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hamzah narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn Waqid narrated to me, that Khalid ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Husayn narrated to him, he said: Abu Hurayrah narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever wears silk in this world will not wear it in the Hereafter, and whoever drinks wine in this world will not drink it in the Hereafter, and whoever drinks in vessels of silver and gold will not drink with them in the Hereafter. Then he said: The clothing of the people of Paradise, and the drink of the people of Paradise, and the vessels of the people of Paradise. So in these successive reports is the prohibition of wearing silk. So it is possible that they abrogated what contains permission to wear it, and possible that what contains permission is the abrogator. So we examined that, to know the abrogating from the abrogated."
"6272","6679 - So Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdur Rahman al-Allaf narrated to us, he said: Ibn Sawaa narrated to us, from Said, from Qatadah, from Anas, that Ukaydir of Dumah gifted to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a jubbah of sundus (fine silk), and that was before he forbade silk, so he wore it, and the people marvelled at it. He said: By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, the handkerchiefs of Sad ibn Muadh in Paradise are better than this."
"6273","6680 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah and al-Layth ibn Sad informed me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu al-Khayr, that he heard Uqbah ibn Amir saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us one day wearing a silk farruj (long coat), so he prayed in it, then he turned away and took it off, and said: This garment is not befitting for the God-fearing."
"6274","6681 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Abdul Hamid ibn Jafar narrated to me, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Habib narrated to us, and he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"6275","6682 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu al-Khayr, from Uqbah ibn Amir, that he said: A silk farruj was gifted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he wore it, then he mentioned the like of it. So these reports indicate that wearing silk was permissible, and that the prohibition of wearing it came after its permissibility, so we knew that what came regarding the prohibition of wearing it is the abrogator of what came regarding the permissibility of wearing it. And this is also the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, Muhammad, and most of the scholars."
"6276","6683 - And there has been narrated from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding that, what Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Sad ibn Ibrahim, from his father, that his uncle Ismail ibn Abdur Rahman entered with Abdur Rahman upon Umar, wearing a shirt of silk and two gold clasps, so he tore the shirt, undid the two clasps, and said: Go to your mother."
"6277","6684 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Misar narrated to us, from Wabarah ibn Abdur Rahman, from Amir, from Suwayd ibn Ghafalah, he said: We came to Umar wearing garments of the people of Persia, or he said: of Kisra, so he said: May Allah disfigure these faces, so we returned and cast them off and wore Arab garments, then we returned to him and he said: You are better than a people who came to me wearing the garments of a people; had Allah been pleased with them for them, He would not have dressed them in them. Nothing is proper, or nothing is lawful, except two fingers or three or four, meaning silk."
"6278","6685 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Sumay, from Muslim al-Batin, from Abu Amr as-Saybani, he said: Ali ibn Abi Talib saw upon a man a jubbah with softness of dibaj at its chest. So Ali said to him: What is this thing under your beard? The man began to look, so a man said to him: He only means the dibaj."
"6279","6686 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, from Safwan ibn Abdullah ibn Safwan, he said: Sad ibn Abi Waqqas sought permission upon Ibn Amir, while beneath him were cushions of silk, so he ordered them removed, then Sad entered upon him wearing a mitraf (cloak), half of it silk. So Ibn Amir said to him: O Abu Ishaq, I sought permission from you while beneath me were cushions of silk, so I ordered them removed. He said: You are a fine man, O Ibn Amir, if you are not among those about whom Allah, mighty and majestic is He, said: You exhausted your good things in your worldly life and enjoyed them. For me to lie upon the embers of ghadha (a tree) is more beloved to me than to lie upon silk cushions. He said: Then this mitraf upon you, half of it khazz (mixed silk) and half silk, he said: Only the khazz touches my skin."
"6280","6687 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Talq ibn Habib, he said: I said to Ibn Umar: What do you say about this silk, is it something you heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), or did you find it in the Book of Allah, mighty and majestic is He? He said: I did not find it in the Book of Allah, nor did I hear it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), but I saw the people of Islam disliking it."
"6281","6688 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Awn, he said: I do not know except that he said from al-Hasan, he said: We entered upon Ibn Umar at al-Batha, and a man said to him: These garments of ours are mixed with silk. He said: Leave it, its little and its much. Abu Jafar said: So some people went to the view that what was forbidden of that includes both women and men, and they argued with the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Whoever wears it in this world will not wear it in the Hereafter, and he did not specify men to the exclusion of women in that. They said: We have seen vessels of gold and silver forbidden to Muslims, because they are the vessels of the disbelievers, so women and men are equal in that. So likewise silk, when it was forbidden to Muslims because it is the clothing of the disbelievers, men and women are all equal in it. So part of the proof against those who held this view is that wearing dyed garments was forbidden, and it was said: They are the clothing of the disbelievers. And there was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding that what..."
"6282","6689 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, from Hisham, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from Khalid ibn Madan, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from Abdullah ibn Amr: That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw upon him two garments dyed with safflower, he said: These are among the garments of the disbelievers, so do not wear them."
"6283","6690 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Harun ibn Ismail al-Khazzaz narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, and he mentioned with his isnad the like of it. So in this hadith, dyed garments are the garments of the disbelievers. So we examined that: was wearing them forbidden for this reason for women or not?"
"6284","6691 - So Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Umarah ibn Zad han narrated to us, from Ziyad an-Numayri, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wearing a garment dyed with safflower, so he said to him: If this garment of yours were in an oven, it would be better for you. So the man went and placed it under the pot, or in the oven, then he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: What did you do with your garment? He said: I did with it as you commanded me. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: I did not command you with this; why did you not cast it upon one of your women? So that prohibition was upon men, to the exclusion of women. And there has been narrated regarding that from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what..."
"6285","6692 - Abu Hazim, Abdul Hamid ibn Abdul Aziz, narrated to us, he said: Bundar narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Adi narrated to us, from Said ibn Abi Arubah, from Abu Mashar, from Ibrahim an-Nakhai, he said: I entered upon Aishah and saw upon her dyed garments."
"6286","6693 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed me, from Musa ibn Uqbah, he said: Umm Salamah, Aishah, and Umm Habibah used to wear garments dyed with safflower."
"6287","6694 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu az-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir saying to his family: Do not wear perfumed garments, and wear garments dyed with safflower without perfume."
"6288","6695 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, that Malik narrated to him, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Asma bint Abi Bakr as-Siddiq, that she used to wear garments dyed with safflower while in ihram, with no saffron in them."
"6289","6696 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Salim informed me, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from Fatimah bint al-Mundhir, that she said: I never saw Asma wear except dyed with safflower, until she met Allah, mighty and majestic is He, and she would wear a garment standing stiff with safflower. So why would they deny that silk is likewise, so its wearing is disliked for men, not disliked for women. If they say to us: Why do you not liken the ruling of wearing silk in this chapter to the ruling of using vessels of gold and silver? It is said to them: Because dyed garments are clothing, and likewise garments of silk and dibaj, while gold and silver are vessels, and clothing resembles clothing more than vessels. And this is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah, exalted is He, have mercy on them. And there has also been narrated regarding that from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what..."
"6290","6697 - Rabi al-Muadh dhin narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu as-Saabah, from a man of Hamdan called Aflah, from Ibn Zurayr, that he heard Ali ibn Abi Talib saying: The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took silk in his right hand, and took gold and placed it in his left, then said: These two are forbidden for the males of my ummah."
"6291","6698 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abdul Aziz ibn Abi as-Saabah, from Abu Aflah, from Abdullah ibn Zurayr al-Ghafiqi, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"6292","6699 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah informed me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abdul Aziz ibn Abi as-Saabah al-Qurashi, from Abu Ali al-Hamdani, from Abdullah ibn Zurayr, he said: I heard Ali ibn Abi Talib saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us with gold in one hand and silk in the other, and said: These two are forbidden for the males of my ummah and lawful for its females."
"6293","6700 - Rabi al-Muadh dhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Habib narrated to us that Abdul Aziz ibn Abi as-Saabah al-Qurashi narrated to him, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"6294","6701 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Abdur Rahman ibn Ziyad ibn Anum informed me, from Abdur Rahman ibn Rafi, from Abdullah ibn Amr, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"6295","6702 - Ibrahim ibn Munqidh and Salih ibn Abdur Rahman narrated to us, they said: al-Muqri narrated to us, from Abdur Rahman ibn Ziyad, and he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"6296","6703 - Ibn Abi Imran, Ibn Abi Dawud, Ali ibn Abdur Rahman, Abu Zurah ad-Dimashqi, and Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, they said: Said ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, from Abbad ibn al-Awwam, he said: Said ibn Abi Arubah narrated to us, he said: Thabit ibn Arqam narrated to me, he said: My paternal aunt Unaysah bint Zayd ibn Arqam narrated to me, from her father Zayd ibn Arqam, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it, and Ali ibn Abdur Rahman added: A man said to him: You say this, while the Commander of the Believers Ali ibn Abi Talib forbids it. She said: And in my hand were two gold clasps, so he said: Put them down, and he mounted his Humayr (donkey) and went, then returned and said: Put them back on, for I asked him and he said: There is no harm in it."
"6297","6704 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Thawban and Amr ibn al-Harith narrated to me, from Hisham ibn Abi Ruqayyah, he said: I heard Maslamah ibn Mukhallad saying to Uqbah ibn Amir: Stand and narrate to the people what you heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), meaning: So Uqbah stood and said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever lies upon me deliberately, let him take his seat of Hell. And I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Silk and gold are forbidden for the males of my ummah, lawful for its females."
"6298","6705 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal al-Anmati narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Said ibn Abi Hind, from Abu Musa al-Ashari, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Silk and gold are lawful for the females of my ummah, forbidden for its males."
"6299","6706 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Said ibn Abi Hind informed me, from his father, from Abu Musa al-Ashari, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. So it is clarified in these reports whom the prohibition in the first reports was aimed at, and that they are men to the exclusion of women. Then the others said: It has been narrated from Ibn Umar and Ibn az-Zubayr that they applied the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Whoever wears silk in this world will not wear it in the Hereafter, to men and women. And they mentioned regarding that what..."
"6300","6707 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Yusuf ibn Mahak, he said: A woman asked Ibn Umar, saying: May I adorn myself with gold? He said: Yes. She said: What do you say to me about silk? He said: That is disliked. She said: What disliked? Inform me, is it lawful or unlawful? He said: We used to narrate that whoever wears it in this world will not wear it in the Hereafter."
"6301","6708 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Nizar narrated to us, he said: Abdul Aziz ibn Abi Rawwad narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that a woman asked him about wearing silk, so he disliked it. She said: Why? He said to her: Since you insist, I shall inform you: We used to say: Whoever wears it in this world will not wear it in the Hereafter."
"6302","6709 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dhibyan informed me, he said: I heard Ibn az-Zubayr delivering a sermon, saying: O people, do not dress your women in silk, for I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever wears silk in this world will not wear it in the Hereafter. Ibn az-Zubayr said: And I say: Whoever does not wear it in the Hereafter will not enter Paradise, because Allah, mighty and majestic is He, said: And their clothing therein will be silk."
"6303","6710 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: al-Azraq ibn Qays al-Harithi narrated to me, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn az-Zubayr delivering a sermon on the Day of Tarwiyah, saying: O people, do not wear silk, nor dress your women and children in it, for whoever wears it in this world will not wear it in the Hereafter. And they mentioned regarding that also, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what..."
"6304","6711 - Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, that Abu Ushshanah al-Maafiri narrated to him, that he heard Uqbah ibn Amir al-Juhani reporting that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to forbid his family ornaments and silk, saying: If you love the ornaments and silk of Paradise, do not wear them in this world. It was said to them: As for the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Whoever wears it in this world will not wear it in the Hereafter, it has indeed been narrated. And it is possible that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) meant by it men specifically, and possible that he meant by it men and women. And what we mentioned of the hadith of Ali, Abdullah ibn Umar, Zayd ibn Arqam, and Abu Musa informs that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) meant by it men, to the exclusion of women, so it is more worthy. And this meaning is more worthy to be taken as the sense of this hadith, so that it does not contradict what we mentioned before it. And even if what they mentioned from Ibn Umar and Ibn az-Zubayr in that is proof, then what we mentioned from Ali contradicting that is more worthy of being proof. And there has also been narrated in this from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), contrary to that."
"6305","6712 - Yazid ibn Sinan and Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: I heard Nafi narrating from Ibn Umar, he said: Umar saw Utarid at-Tamimi displaying a striped silk suit in the market. So Umar said: O Messenger of Allah, if only you would buy it for the Arab delegations when they come to you? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Silk in this world is worn only by one who has no share in the Hereafter. Then after that, striped suits were brought to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he sent to Umar a suit, and to Usamah a suit, and he gave Ali a suit and commanded him to cut it into headcovers among his women. He said: And Usamah went out in his suit, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked at it with a look by which he knew he disliked what he had done, and said: I did not send it to you to wear it; I only sent it to you to cut it into headcovers among your women."
"6306","6713 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Hamid ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Ayyub ibn Musa narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw a striped silk suit upon Utarid, so he disliked it for him and forbade him from it, then he clothed Umar with the like of it. He said: O Messenger of Allah, you said about the suit of Utarid what you said, yet you clothe me with this? He said: I did not clothe you to wear it; I only gave it to you to dress women in it. So Ibn Umar reported, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this hadith, that his saying: Silk in this world is worn only by one who has no share, he meant by it men to the exclusion of women, and this has been narrated from Ali, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6307","6714 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Waki narrated to us, from Misar, from Abu Awn, from Abu Salih al-Hanafi, from Ali, that Ukaydir of Dumah gifted to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a garment of silk, so he gave it to him and said: Cut it into headcovers among the women. And there was narrated from Ali ibn Abi Talib regarding that what..."
"6308","6715 - Abu Bakrah and Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Abu Dawud at-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Awn ath-Thaqafi, he said: I heard Abu Salih al-Hanafi saying: I heard Ali saying: A striped silk suit was gifted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he sent it to me and I wore it, then I saw dislike in his face, so I cut it into headcovers among my women."
"6309","6716 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Abdur Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: Abu Awn, Muhammad ibn Abdullah, informed me, and he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"6310","6717 - Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abdur Rahman narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abdul Malik ibn Maysarah, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from Ali, and he mentioned the like of it."
"6311","6718 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, that Ibrahim ibn Abdullah ibn Hunayn narrated to him: That his father narrated to him: That he heard Ali ibn Abi Talib saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) clothed me with a striped silk suit, so I went out in it. He said to me: O Ali, I did not clothe you to wear it. So I returned to Fatimah, may Allah be pleased with her, and gave her its edge, as if it were folded with me, then I cut it, and she said: May your hands be dusted, O son of Abu Talib, what have you brought? I said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade me to wear it, so wear it yourself and clothe your women."
"6312","6719 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Imran ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Abu Fakhitah, from Jaadah, from Ali, he said: The chief of Adharbayjan gifted to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a suit striped with silk, either its warp or its weft, so he sent it to me and I came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, shall I wear it? He said: No, I dislike for you what I dislike for myself, but make it headcovers among the Fatimahs. He said: So I cut from it four headcovers: a headcover for Fatimah bint Asad ibn Hashim, mother of Ali ibn Abi Talib, and a headcover for Fatimah bint the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and a headcover for Fatimah bint Hamzah ibn Abdul Muttalib, and a headcover for another Fatimah I have forgotten."
"6313","6720 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: al-Qanabi narrated to us, he said: Abdul Aziz ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Abu Fakhitah, from Jaadah ibn Hubayrah, from Ali, that a suit whose weft or warp was silk was gifted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So I said: O Messenger of Allah, shall I wear it? He said: No, I dislike for you what I dislike for myself, but cut it into headcovers for so-and-so, so-and-so, and so-and-so, and he mentioned Fatimah among them. He said: So I cut it into four headcovers."
"6314","6721 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, he said: I heard Mujahid narrating from Ibn Abi Layla, he said: I heard Ali saying: A silk suit was brought to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he sent it to me and I wore it, then I saw dislike in his face, so I cut it into headcovers among the women. And there has been narrated regarding that from Anas ibn Malik what..."
"6315","6722 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn Abi Hamzah narrated to us, from az-Zuhri, from Anas, that he saw upon Umm Kulthum, daughter of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), a striped silk cloak."
"6316","6723 - Muhammad ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hamzah narrated to us, from az-Zubaydi, from az-Zuhri, from Anas, the like of it."
"6317","6724 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Jafar ar-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Yusuf narrated to us, from al-Awzai and Mamar, from az-Zuhri, from Anas, the like of it."
"6318","6725 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Khattab ibn Uthman and Haywah ibn Shurayh narrated to us, he said: Baqiyyah narrated to us, from az-Zubaydi, from az-Zuhri, from Anas, the like of it. He said: And as-sira is that striped with qazz (raw silk)."
"6319","6726 - Salih ibn Abdur Rahman narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Mamar, from az-Zuhri, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: I saw upon Zaynab, daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), a striped cloak of silk. So it is established by these reports, of what we presented of examination, the permissibility of wearing silk for women, and this is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them. And also..."
"6320","6727 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Misar narrated to us, from Abdul Malik ibn Maysarah, from Amr ibn Dinar, that Jabir ibn Abdullah removed silk from the boy and left it upon the girls. Misar said: And I asked Amr ibn Dinar about it, but he did not recognize it."
"6321","6728 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: al-Qasim ibn Malik al-Muzani narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Humayd ibn Abdur Rahman, from Sad ibn Hisham, he said: Aishah narrated to me, she said: We had a qatifah (blanket) whose border was silk, and we used to wear it."
"6322","6729 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from al-Mughirah ibn Ziyad, from Abu Umar, freed slave of Asma, he said: I saw Ibn Umar buy a jubbah with a red thread in it, so he returned it. So I came to Asma and mentioned that to her. She said: Woe to Ibn Umar; O slave-girl, hand me the jubbah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So she brought out to us a jubbah edged at the opening, sleeves, and front with dibaj."
"6323","6730 - al-Husayn ibn Abdullah ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: al-Haytham ibn Jamil narrated to us. And: 6731 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Khusayf, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only forbade the garment wholly of silk, while as for the warp and the border, no."
"6324","6732 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, from Khusayf, and he mentioned with his isnad the like of it. So in these reports is permission to wear a garment not of silk when it contains silk like a border, or when its weft is not silk though its warp is silk. And among what indicates the soundness of what they said of that is what has been narrated from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of their wearing khazz."
"6325","6733 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Muhajir narrated to us, he said: I heard my father mentioning from ash-Shabi, he said: I saw upon al-Husayn ibn Ali a jubbah of khazz."
"6326","6734 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq narrated to us, from al-Ayzar ibn Hurayth, he said: I saw upon al-Husayn ibn Ali a mitraf of khazz."
"6327","6735 - Ali ibn Abdur Rahman narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Bakr ibn Mudar narrated to us, from Amr ibn al-Harith, from Bukayr ibn Abdullah, that Busr ibn Said narrated to him that he saw upon Sad ibn Abi Waqqas a Syrian jubbah whose warp was qazz (raw silk). Busr said: And I saw upon Zayd ibn Thabit bordered khamais (woollen cloaks)."
"6328","6736 - Ali narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Main narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Umar narrated to us, from Wahb ibn Kaysan, he said: I saw Sad ibn Abi Waqqas, Abu Hurayrah, Jabir ibn Abdullah, and Anas ibn Malik wearing khazz."
"6329","6737 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Malik informed me, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, that she clothed Abdullah ibn az-Zubayr with a mitraf of khazz which Aishah used to wear."
"6330","6738 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ammar ibn Abi Ammar, freed slave of Banu Hashim, he said: Khazz matarif (cloaks) came to Marwan ibn al-Hakam, so he clothed some of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with them, and it is as if I am looking at Abu Hurayrah with a dusty mitraf of them upon him, as if I am looking at the streaks of silk in it."
"6331","6739 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Salih ibn Hatim ibn Wardan narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Awn narrated to me, he said: I saw upon Anas ibn Malik a jubbah of khazz, a mitraf of khazz, and a turban of khazz."
"6332","6740 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Mahdi ibn Maymun narrated to us, from Shuayb ibn al-Habhab, he said: I saw upon Anas ibn Malik a jubbah of khazz, and a mitraf of khazz, or he said: and a burnus of khazz."
"6333","6741 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ziyad, that he saw upon Abu Hurayrah a mitraf of khazz. So these Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to wear khazz, whose warp is silk. And part of the proof of the others against the people of this view was that khazz in those days had no silk in it. So it is said to them: What is your proof for what you mentioned, though we mentioned in some of these reports that the jubbah of Sad had its warp of qazz. And we narrated from him in this book of ours, in other than this chapter, that he entered upon Ibn Amir wearing a jubbah, half of it khazz and half silk. So Ibn Amir spoke to him about that, and he said: Only the khazz touches my skin. So that indicates that their khazz was like the khazz of people after them, containing silk and containing khazz. So with the establishment of that is established what those who permitted wearing a garment not of silk bordered with silk went to, and wearing a garment whose warp is silk while its outer is not silk. And this is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them."
"6334","7 - Chapter on a man whose tooth moves: may he fasten it with gold or not? Abu Jafar said: People differed about a man whose tooth moves and he wishes to fasten it with gold. Abu Hanifah said: He may not do that, nor fasten it with silver likewise. 6742 - Muhammad ibn al-Abbas narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, from Abu Yusuf, from Abu Hanifah. And the companions of dictation (ashab al-imla), among them Bishr ibn al-Walid, from Abu Yusuf, from Abu Hanifah, that there is no harm in fastening it with gold. And Muhammad ibn al-Hasan said: There is no harm in fastening it with gold, likewise. And part of the proof for Abu Hanifah, in his saying which Muhammad narrated from Abu Yusuf from him, is that gold and silk were forbidden, so using them was forbidden, and what was forbidden of silk includes wearing it and binding wounds with it. So likewise what was forbidden of using gold includes fastening the tooth with it. And part of the proof for Muhammad in what he held of that against Abu Hanifah in his narration from Abu Yusuf from him is that what was mentioned of binding wounds with silk, if it was done because it is treatment for wounds, there is no harm in it, because that is medicine, just as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted az-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam and Abdur Rahman ibn Awf to wear silk due to the itch that was in them; likewise silk bandages, if they are treatment for the wound shortening its duration, just as the silk garment is treatment for itching, there is no harm in them, and if it is not treatment for the wound, then they and all other bandages are alike in that, so they are disliked. So likewise what we mentioned of gold: if what is intended of it is that it does not stink as silver stinks, there is no harm in it, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted Arfajah ibn Asad to take a nose of gold."
"6335","6743 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ashhab narrated to us. And: 6744 - Abu Bishr ar-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Ghassan ibn Ubayd al-Musalli narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ashhab narrated to us. And: 6745 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ashhab narrated to us, from Abdur Rahman ibn Tarafah, from his grandfather Arfajah ibn Asad, that his nose was injured on the Day of Kulab in Jahiliyyah, so he took a nose of silver (wariq), but it stank upon him, so he complained of that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he commanded him to take a nose of gold, and he did."
"6336","6746 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Abdur Rahman ibn Ziyad, al-Khasib ibn Nasih, and Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, they said: Abu al-Ashhab narrated to us, from Abdur Rahman ibn Tarafah, from Arfajah, the like of it. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted Arfajah ibn Asad to take a nose of gold when silver stinks. So since that is so for the nose, so too the tooth: he should not fasten it with gold when other than it does not stink, so the stink from silver permits using gold, just as the stink from it in the nose permitted using gold in its place; this is a proof. And in that is another proof: we saw using silver disliked as using gold is disliked. So since they are equal in dislike, and prohibition covered them both, and fastening the tooth with silver is outside the disliked use, so too fastening it with gold is outside the disliked use. If one says: We saw the silver ring permitted for men, while they were barred from the gold ring, so what was permitted to them of silver was not permitted to them of gold. It is said to him: Reasoning was as we narrated, namely permitting the gold ring for men like the silver ring. But we were barred from that, and the prohibition of the gold ring came as a text, so we held by it and left reasoning for it; had it not been for that, we would have made it in permissibility like the silver ring. So likewise fastening the tooth: since it was permitted with silver, it is established that fastening it with gold is likewise, until a sunnah comes differentiating between that requiring leaving reasoning, just as in the gold ring a sunnah came forbidding it, completing the proof, requiring leaving reasoning for it. So established by what we mentioned is what Muhammad said. If one says: What has been narrated forbidding the gold ring? It is said to him: Successive reports have been narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that, coming soundly, and we shall mention them in the chapter on forbidding the gold ring, if Allah, exalted is He, wills. And it has been narrated from a group of the predecessors permitting fastening teeth with gold."
"6337","6747 - So among that is what Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan and Musa ibn Dawud narrated to us, they said: Tumah ibn Amr narrated to us, he said: I saw the yellowness of gold between the front teeth, or he said: between the two front teeth, of Musa ibn Talhah."
"6338","6748 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Humayd at-Tawil, he said: I saw al-Hasan fasten his teeth with gold."
"6339","6749 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ashhab narrated to us, from Hammad, he said: I saw al-Mughirah ibn Abdullah, governor of Kufah, had clasped his teeth with gold. So I mentioned that to Ibrahim, and he said: There is no harm in it."
"6340","6750 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Abdur Rahman narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: I saw Abu at-Tayyah, Abu Hamzah, and Abu Nawfal ibn Abi Aqrab had clasped their teeth with gold."
"6341","6751 - Sulayman narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: I saw Ubaydullah ibn al-Hasan, judge of Basrah, had fastened his teeth with gold. So what we narrated from them of this agrees with what Muhammad ibn al-Hasan held in it; we take it."
"6342","8 - Chapter on wearing gold rings. 6752 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Abu Raja narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Malik, he said: I saw upon al-Bara a ring of gold, so it was said to him. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) distributed booty and dressed me in it, saying: Wear what Allah and His Messenger have dressed you. Abu Jafar said: So a group went to permitting wearing gold rings for men, and they argued with this hadith for that. And they said: It has been narrated from a group of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that they used to wear gold rings."
"6343","6753 - So they mentioned regarding that what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Qawariri narrated to us, he said: Ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Muhammad, from Musab ibn Sad, he said: I saw in the hand of Talhah ibn Ubaydullah a ring of gold, and I saw in the hand of Suhayb a ring of gold, and I saw in the hand of Sad a ring of gold."
"6344","6754 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: an-Nadr ibn Abdul Jabbar narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Zayd, from Isa ibn Talhah, that he informed him that Talhah ibn Ubaydullah was killed with a ring of gold in his hand."
"6345","6755 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Jafar ibn Rabiah, from Ibn Shihab, from Yahya ibn Said ibn al-As, that Said ibn al-As was killed with a ring of gold in his hand."
"6346","6756 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Mighwal narrated to us, he said: Abu as-Safar narrated to us."
"6347","6757 - And Ali narrated to us, he said: Khallad ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Abu as-Safar narrated to us, he said: I saw upon al-Bara a ring of gold. So they went to following these reports, along with what they attached to that of the hadith of al-Bara which we mentioned at the start of this chapter. And for them in that of reasoning is that he forbade using gold and silver with one prohibition, and barred eating in silver vessels as he barred eating in gold vessels. So since he equated gold and silver in that and made their ruling one, then it being established that the silver ring is not what he forbade, so too the gold ring. And others opposed them in that, and disliked gold rings for men."
"6348","6758 - And they argued in that with what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Nafi informed me, from Dawud ibn Qays, from Ibrahim ibn Abdullah ibn Hunayn, from his father, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us wearing gold rings."
"6349","6759 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ajlan, he said: Ibrahim ibn Abdullah ibn Hunayn narrated to me, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, from Ali, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"6350","6760 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, that Malik narrated to him, from Nafi, from Ibrahim ibn Abdullah ibn Hunayn, from his father, from Ali, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"6351","6761 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Qays narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Abdullah ibn Hunayn, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, from Ali, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"6352","6762 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us. And: 6763 - Rabi al-Muadh dhin narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, they said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, that Ibrahim ibn Abdullah ibn Hunayn narrated to him that his father narrated to him that he heard Ali saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade me the gold ring."
"6353","6764 - Rabi al-Muadh dhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Hubayrah ibn Maryam, from Ali, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the gold ring."
"6354","6765 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not wear a gold ring."
"6355","6766 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: an-Nufayli narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad narrated to us, from Abu Said al-Azdi, from Abu al-Kanud, he said: I came to Abdullah ibn Masud and he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the gold ring."
"6356","6767 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Yazid, and he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"6357","6768 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan informed us, he said: Ibn Ajlan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather, that a man sat with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wearing a ring of gold, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned away from him, so he wore an iron ring, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This is the dress of the people of Hell. So he returned and wore a ring of silver (wariq), and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was silent toward him."
"6358","6769 - Abdul Ghani ibn Rifaah narrated to us, he said: Abdur Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us. And: 6770 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Ashath ibn Abi ash-Shatha, from Muawiyah ibn Suwayd ibn Muqarrin, from al-Bara ibn Azib, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the gold ring. So this al-Bara, we have narrated from him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this, contrary to what we narrated from him at the start of this chapter."
"6359","6771 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: Abu at-Tayyah narrated to us, he said: I heard a man of Banu Layth saying: I bear witness upon Imran ibn Husayn that he narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he forbade the gold ring."
"6360","6772 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Abu at-Tayyah, from Hafs al-Laythi, from Imran ibn Husayn, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"6361","6773 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Shubah informed me, from Qatadah, from an-Nadr ibn Anas, from Bishr ibn Nahik, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the gold ring."
"6362","6774 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: I heard an-Numan ibn Rashid narrating from az-Zuhri, from Ata ibn Yazid, from Abu Thalabah al-Khushani, he said: A man sat with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wearing a ring of gold, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) struck his hand with a stick in his hand, then he was heedless of him, so the man threw his ring away, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked at him and said: Where is your ring? He said: I cast it away. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I think we have pained you and cost you."
"6363","6775 - Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah informed me, from Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah al-Ansari, from Sumayy, freed slave of Abu Bakr, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, that a man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wearing a ring of gold, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned away from him. So he went and wore a ring of iron, then came, and he turned away from him, so he went and took it off and wore a ring of silver, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) approved him and turned to him. So these reports have been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding wearing gold rings. Among them is the hadith of al-Bara which we mentioned therein, and it is sounder and firmer than what we narrated from him of permission. So it is possible that what one of the two parties held from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) abrogates what the other party narrated."
"6364","6776 - So we examined that, and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Ubaydullah, he said: Nafi narrated to me, from Abdullah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took a ring of gold, with most of its stone toward his palm, so the people took it, then he cast it away and took a ring of silver."
"6365","6777 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"6366","6778 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: al-Qanabi narrated to us, he said: I read to Malik ibn Anas, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to wear a ring of gold, then he stood and cast it away, saying: I shall never wear it, so the people cast away their rings."
"6367","6779 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, from Ali ibn Mabad, from Ismail ibn Jafar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"6368","6780 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from al-Mughirah ibn Ziyad, that he narrated to him, he said: Nafi narrated to me, from Ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took a ring of gold, so his Companions took rings of gold, then he cast it away and took a ring of silver, inscribing in it: Muhammad, Messenger of Allah."
"6369","6781 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abdul Wahid ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. So it is established by these reports that wearing gold rings was permissible, then he forbade it after that. So it is established that what contains the prohibition of wearing them is the abrogator of what contains the permission of wearing them. So this is the aspect of this chapter by way of reports. As for reasoning in that, we mentioned it in what preceded of our mention of it, in other than this place, and that it agrees with what those who held to permission held. But the sunnah in that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), forbidding that, prohibited it and barred from it."
"6370","6782 - And among what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding that also is what Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, freed slave of Ibn Umar, from Hunayn, freed slave of Ibn Abbas, from Ali, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he forbade him wearing gold rings."
"6371","6783 - Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Ibrahim ibn Abdullah ibn Hunayn, from his father, from Ali, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. If one says: Do you find from any of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a prohibition in that? It is said to him: Yes."
"6372","6784 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abdur Rahman, freed slave of Umm Burthun, from Ziyad, governor of Basrah, he said: We came as a delegation to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, with al-Ashari, and he saw upon me a ring of gold. So Umar said: You have resembled the non-Arabs, thrice saying it: Wear rings of this silver. He said: al-Ashari said: As for me, my ring is iron, so Umar said: That is more wicked and foul-smelling."
"6373","9 - Chapter on engraving rings. 6785 - Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn as-Sabbah narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from al-Awwam ibn Hawshab, from al-Azhar ibn Rashid, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not seek light from the fires of the polytheists, nor engrave Arabic. He said: So I asked al-Hasan about that, and he said: His saying: Do not engrave Arabic, means do not engrave upon your rings: Muhammad, Messenger of Allah. And his saying: Do not seek light from the fires of the polytheists, means: Do not consult them in your affairs. Abu Jafar said: So a group went to disliking engraving rings with anything of Arabic, and they argued with this hadith for that. And they saw no harm in engraving other than Arabic, arguing with what was upon the rings of some of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6374","6786 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Mualla narrated to us, from Mansur, he said: Abdul Wahid ibn Ziyad informed me, he said: Umm Nafi, daughter of Abu al-Jad, freed slave of an-Numan ibn Muqarrin, narrated to us, from her father, he said: The engraving of the ring of an-Numan ibn Muqarrin was a camel holding one hand closed and the other open."
"6375","6787 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Jad narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Jabir, from al-Qasim, he said: The engraving of the ring of Abdullah was two flies."
"6376","6788 - Ali narrated to us, he said: Ali narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Musa ibn Abdullah ibn Yazid, from his father, he said: The engraving of the ring of Hudhayfah was two cranes. And others opposed them in that, saying: There is no harm in engraving Arabic upon rings, other than what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) barred of engraving upon his ring. And they said: There is no proof for the people of the first view in what they argued with in that, because their hadith which they narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is not established by way of isnad, but its origin is from Umar, not from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6377","6789 - And they mentioned in that what Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Surayj ibn an-Numan narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Do not engrave Arabic in your rings. So this is the origin of this hadith of Anas, from Umar, not from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then even if it were established from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), its interpretation with us would be what al-Hasan said, because the engraving of the ring of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was likewise, so he forbade engraving upon it."
"6378","6790 - Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Khushaysh narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Ansari narrated to us, from his father, from Thumamah, from Anas, he said: The engraving of the ring of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was three lines: a line Muhammad, a line Messenger, and a line Allah. So this was the engraving of the ring of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6379","6791 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Abdul Wahhab narrated to us, he said: Said narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended to write to Kisra and Qaysar. So it was said to him: They will not accept your letter except with a seal, so he took a ring of silver, its engraving: Muhammad, Messenger of Allah."
"6380","6792 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Shababab narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended to write a letter to the Romans, then he mentioned the like of it. So this Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) engraved Arabic in his ring, then his Companions did that after him."
"6381","6793 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Qurashi narrated to us, from Amr ibn Yahya, from his grandfather who said: Amr ibn Said came with his brother to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he saw a ring on his hand and said: What is this ring on your hand? He said: This is a ring, O Messenger of Allah. He said: What is its inscription? He said: Muhammad, Messenger of Allah. He said: Show it to me. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wore it on his finger, and he died while it was on his hand, then Abu Bakr took it after that and it was on his hand, then Umar took it and it was on his hand, then Uthman took it and it was on his hand for most of his caliphate, until it fell from him into the well of Aris. So this Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not object to Khalid ibn Said wearing what is inscribed in Arabic."
"6382","6794 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Jad narrated to us, he said: al-Rabi ibn Subayh narrated to us, from Hayyan al-Saigh who said: The inscription of the ring of Abu Bakr al-Siddiq was: How excellent is Allah, the All-Powerful."
"6383","6795 - Ali narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Amr narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, from Jabir, from Abu Jafar who said: The inscription of the ring of Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, was: To Allah belongs sovereignty."
"6384","6796 - Ali narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from Qatada who said: The inscription of the ring of Abu Ubayda ibn al-Jarrah was: Praise be to Allah. So these Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the Rightly Guided Caliphs engraved Arabic upon their rings. So what they did shows it is not prohibited for them, and the prohibition was only intended so that nothing be engraved upon the ring of the Imam, lest forgery occur regarding the funds of the Muslims in his hands. Do you not see that Umar, from whom we narrated prohibition of that, himself later wore after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what was inscribed in Arabic. So that shows what was disliked of Arabic is only the Arabic placed upon the ring of the Imam of the Muslims specifically, not anything else. As for what was narrated of the inscription of the ring of al-Numan ibn Muqarrin, Ibn Masud and Hudhayfa, it is possible they did that, and they were permitted to engrave Arabic in their place."
"6385","6797 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to me, he said: al-Qawariri narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Warith narrated to us, from Amr, from al-Hasan that he disliked a man engraving an image upon his ring. And he said: When you seal with it, you have made an image with it."
"6386","10 - Chapter on wearing a ring for one without authority. 6798 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Mualla ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Mufaddal ibn Fudala narrated to us, he said: Ayyash ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from al-Haytham ibn Shafi al-Hajari, from Abu Amir, from Abu Rayhana who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade wearing a ring except for one with authority. Abu Jafar said: So a group went to disliking wearing a ring except for one with authority, and argued with this hadith. Others opposed them and saw no harm in others wearing it, whether ruler or not. Part of their proof was the hadith we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the previous chapter, that he cast away his ring, so the people cast away their rings. This shows the general people wore rings in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). If one says: how do you argue with this when it is abrogated? It is said: what we argue with from it is not abrogated; only the abandonment of wearing gold rings, for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and others of his ummah, is abrogated. Before that, he and they were alike in that. When wearing gold rings was abrogated, the ruling remained the same for his wearing and their wearing of rings, and just as abrogation did not prevent him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from wearing a silver ring, likewise it does not prevent them from wearing silver rings. This is what we intended from this hadith. And it was narrated from a group who had no authority that they wore rings."
"6387","6799 - From what was narrated of that: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jafar al-Madaini narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Ismail narrated to us, from Jafar ibn Muhammad, from his father, that al-Hasan and al-Husayn used to wear rings on their left hands, and in their rings was the remembrance of Allah."
"6388","6800 - Ali narrated to us, he said: Yala ibn Ubayd narrated to us, he said: Rashdin ibn Kurayb narrated to us that he said: I saw Ibn al-Hanafiyya wearing a ring on his left hand."
"6389","6801 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wuhazi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, he said: Jafar ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from his father who said: al-Hasan and al-Husayn used to wear rings on their left hands."
"6390","6802 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Ata, from his father who said: The inscription of the ring of Imran ibn Husayn was a man girt with a sword."
"6391","6803 - Ali narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Amr narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq narrated to us, he said: I saw Qays ibn Abi Hazim, Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad, Qays ibn Thumama and al-Shabi wearing rings on their left hands."
"6392","6804 - Ali narrated to me, he said: Ali ibn al-Jad narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from Mughira who said: The inscription of the ring of Ibrahim was: We are by Allah and for Him. So these whom we narrated these reports from, among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and their Successors, used to wear rings though they had no authority. This is the aspect of this chapter by way of reports. As for by way of reasoning, if the ruler may wear a ring because it is not an adornment, then likewise others than the ruler may wear it because it is not an adornment. And we see what was forbidden of using gold and silver applies equally to ruler and common people. So by analogy, what was permitted to the ruler of wearing a ring applies equally to him and the common people. And if the ring was permitted for his need to seal the wealth of the Muslims with it, it is also permitted for the common people for their need to seal their wealth and letters, so there is no difference between ruler and non-ruler in that."
"6393","11 - Chapter on urinating standing. 6805 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us. 6806 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, both said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Miqdam ibn Shurayh, from his father, from Aisha who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never urinated standing since the Quran was revealed to him. Abu Jafar said: So a group disliked urinating standing, and argued with this hadith. Others opposed them and saw no harm in it."
"6394","6807 - And they argued with what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Wail Shaqiq ibn Salama, from Hudhayfa who said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) urinate while standing at the rubbish heap of a people, then water for ablution was brought and he performed ablution and wiped over his leather socks."
"6395","6808 - Abu Bakra and Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, both said: Said ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from Sulayman, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"6396","6809 - Abu Bakra narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Abu Awana narrated to us, from Sulayman, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"6397","6810 - Abu Bakra narrated to us, he said: Muammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, he said: Mansur narrated to us, from Abu Wail, from Hudhayfa, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. So in this hadith is permission to urinate standing, and this is more worthy than what we mentioned before from Aisha. For the hadith of Aisha is only: whoever tells you that the Messenger of Allah urinated standing after the Quran was revealed to him, do not believe him. That is, when the Quran was revealed to him he was commanded therein with purity and avoiding impurity and guarding against it. When Aisha saw that and knew the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) magnifying the command of Allah, and it was predominant with her that one who urinates standing hardly remains safe from urine touching his clothes and body, she said that, and there is in it no report from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) agreeing with that. Then Hudhayfa came and reported that he saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Madinah after the revelation of the Quran to him urinating standing. So thereby permission to urinate standing is established, when the one urinating is safe from impurity upon his body and clothes. And there was narrated from Aisha on this what shows what we held of the meaning of her hadith which we mentioned."
"6398","6811 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, and he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from al-Miqdam ibn Shurayh, from his father, from Aisha who said: Whoever tells you that he saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) urinating standing, belie him, for I saw him urinating sitting. So in this hadith is what shows what Aisha used to repel the report of the sighting of one who saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) urinating standing, and her sighting of him was only urinating sitting. With us there is no proof in this hadith for that, for it is possible he urinated sitting at one time and standing at another time, and she did not report from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this anything showing dislike of urinating standing. And it was narrated from more than one of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he urinated standing."
"6399","6812 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Amir narrated to us, from Shuba that he narrated from Sulayman, from Zayd ibn Wahb who said: I saw Umar urinate standing and he bent over until he almost fell."
"6400","6813 - Abu Bakra narrated to us, he said: Wahb and Abu Dawud narrated to us, both said: Shuba narrated to us, from Salama ibn Kuhayl, from Abu Zibyan that he saw Ali urinate standing."
"6401","6814 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from Sulayman, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"6402","6815 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, from al-Amash, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"6403","6816 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn al-Yaman narrated to us, from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Qabisa ibn Dhuayb who said: I saw Zayd ibn Thabit urinate standing."
"6404","6817 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Man ibn Isa narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar that he said: I saw Abd Allah ibn Umar urinate standing. So these Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to urinate standing, and that with us is upon that they were safe from anything of that touching their clothes and bodies. If one says: it was narrated from Umar ibn al-Khattab what contradicts what you narrated from him in this chapter."
"6405","6818 - Then he mentioned what Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Idris narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar who said: Umar said: I have not urinated standing since I became Muslim. It was said to him: It is possible Umar had not urinated standing since he became Muslim until he said this statement, then he urinated after that standing, as Zayd ibn Wahb narrated from him. In that is what shows he did not see harm in urinating standing. And what we narrated from Ibn Umar in this chapter of his urinating standing also shows that. And he narrated from Umar ibn al-Khattab what we mentioned. So that shows the return of Umar from disliking urinating standing, if that was what Abd Allah ibn Umar narrated from him. And Abd Allah ibn Umar would not leave what he heard from Umar except for what was worthier with him than that."
"6406","12 - Chapter on adjuration (qasam). 6819 - Ishaq ibn al-Husayn al-Tahhan narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyayna narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Yazid, from Ibn Shihab, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utba, from Ibn Abbas in a long hadith, in it mention of a dream which Abu Bakr interpreted in the presence of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Did I get it right, O Messenger of Allah? He said: You got part right and part wrong. He said: I adjure you, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Do not adjure. Abu Jafar said: So a group went to disliking adjuration, and said: it is not fitting for anyone to adjure over anything, and they deemed it grave. Among those who deemed it grave was al-Layth ibn Sad; more than one of our companions mentioned to me, from Isa ibn Hammad Zughba who said: I came to Bakr ibn Mudar to visit him in illness, then al-Layth came and intended to go up to him. Bakr said to him: I adjure you to do it. Al-Layth said to him: Do you know what adjuration is? Do you know what adjuration is? Do you know what adjuration is? Others opposed them and saw no harm in adjuration, and made it an oath, and judged for it the ruling of an oath. And they said: Allah mentioned in more than one place in His Book, the Mighty and Majestic said: Nay! I swear by the Day of Resurrection, and nay I swear by the self-reproaching soul, and He said: Nay! I swear by the places of the stars, and He said: Nay! I swear by this city. The interpretation of that with all scholars was: I swear by the Day of Resurrection, with la as connective. And Allah the Mighty and Majestic said: And they swore by Allah their strongest oaths: Allah will not raise who dies; yes, a true promise upon Him. So He did not blame them for their swearing, and refuted their disbelief, saying: Yes, a true promise upon Him. And in His mentioning their strongest oaths is proof that that swearing from them was an oath. And Allah the Mighty and Majestic said: When they swore they would surely harvest it in the morning. So He did not blame that upon them. Then He said: And they made no exception."
"6407","6820 - Then Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to me, from his father, from Muhammad ibn al-Hasan who said: In this verse is proof that adjuration is an oath, for exception is only in an oath. And when it is an oath, it is permitted in what other oaths are permitted in, and disliked in what other oaths are disliked in. With us there is no proof against the people of this view in the hadith of Ibn Abbas which we mentioned, for it is possible that what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disliked in the adjuration for Abu Bakr was because the interpretation which he got partly right and partly wrong was not from him from revelation, but from what he interprets of dreams for him, just as he forbade intercourse with pregnant women out of fear it would harm their children. When it reached him that Persia and Rome do that and it does not harm their children, he lifted what he had prohibited of that."
"6408","6821 - And as he said regarding pollination of date palms: I do not think that avails anything, so they left it and turned away from it, and that reached the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he said: It was only an opinion I opined; if it avails anything let them do it, for I am only a human like you, and it was only an opinion I opined, and opinion errs and is right, but what I said: Allah the Mighty and Majestic said, I will never lie about Allah. Yazid ibn Sinan narrated that to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, from Simak, from Musa ibn Talha, from his father. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reported that what he said by way of opinion, he is like other humans in their opinions, and what he says from Allah the Mighty and Majestic, that is what opposing is not permitted. And dreams are only interpreted by opinion and striving, and that was narrated from Muhammad ibn Sirin, and he argued with the word of Allah the Mighty and Majestic: And he said to the one whom he thought would be saved of the two. When interpretation was from this aspect in which there is no certainty, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disliked for Abu Bakr to adjure him to inform him of what he thinks is right, upon that it is with him so, though in reality it may be otherwise. Do you not see that if a man looks into a question of fiqh and strives, and his striving leads him to something, it is wide for him to state it and refute what opposes it and declare its sayer wrong, when the proofs by which the answer is derived therein support him. And if he swore that that answer is correct, he would be wrong, for he was not charged with hitting correctness so that what he said is correctness, but he was charged with striving. And striving may lead him to correctness and to other than correctness. From this aspect the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disliked for Abu Bakr swearing upon him to inform him of his correctness what it is, not from disliking adjuration. And there was narrated on that what shows what we mentioned."
"6409","6822 - Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah, from Ibn Abbas, like the hadith of Ishaq ibn al-Husayn, except he said: By Allah you shall inform me of what I got right of what I got wrong. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not adjure. So that shows what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disliked was swearing upon informing him of his right or wrong in something the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had not said by revelation by which he knows the reality of things, not for his mentioning adjuration."
"6410","6823 - And Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith, from Ibn Abbas who said: Adjuration is an oath. So this Ibn Abbas, who narrated the first hadith from him, made adjuration an oath, and in that is proof of permission to swear by it and that with him it is like other oaths. So thereby what we interpreted the first hadith upon is established, and the view of one who interpreted it upon other than what we interpreted it upon is negated."
"6411","6824 - Abu Jafar said: And there was narrated on permission of adjuration what Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi Aqil narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from Ashath ibn Sulaym, from Muawiya ibn Suwayd ibn Muqarrin, from al-Bara ibn Azib who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us to fulfill adjurations."
"6412","6825 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud and Wahb narrated to us, both said: Shuba narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it. Except he said: fulfilling adjuration. Do you not see that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded fulfilling adjuration, and had the adjurer been disobedient his adjuration would not have deserved to be fulfilled."
"6413","6826 - And Abu Bakra and Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, both said: Abd Allah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi narrated to us, he said: Humayd al-Tawil narrated to us, from Anas ibn Malik who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Among the servants of Allah are those who, if they adjured Allah, He would fulfill it. Had adjuration been disliked, its sayer would be disobedient, and Allah would not fulfill the adjuration of one who disobeyed Him. And we narrated earlier in this book of ours, from al-Mughira ibn Shuba that he said: I prayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he found the smell of garlic. When he finished prayer he said: Whoever ate of this tree, let him not approach us in our mosque until its smell goes. Then I came to him and said: I adjure you, O Messenger of Allah, to give me your hand. So he gave it to me, and I showed him bandages on my chest. He said: You have an excuse, and he did not object to his adjuring him."
"6414","6827 - Jafar ibn Sulayman al-Nawfali narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Abi Bakr al-Mawsili narrated to us, from Ibn Abi al-Zinad, from his father, from Amra, from Aisha that she said: Meat was gifted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he said: Gift to Zaynab bint Jahsh. She said: So I gifted to her and she returned it. He said: I adjure you, do not return it. So she returned it. So what we mentioned shows permission of adjuration, and that its ruling is the ruling of an oath, and it is the view of Abu Hanifa, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them. And that was narrated from Ibrahim al-Nakhai."
"6415","6828 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, from Abu Hanifa, from Hammad, from Ibrahim who said: (I swear) and (I swore by Him) is an oath, and its expiation is the expiation of an oath."
"6416","6829 - And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) adjured his wives. Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Hafs al-Fallas narrated to us, he said: Abu Qutayba narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Rijal narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, from Amra, from Aisha who said: The ila of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was: I swear by Allah I shall not approach you for a month."
"6417","13 - Chapter on drinking standing. 6830 - Ibn Abi Imran and Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, both said: Ishaq ibn Ismail al-Talqani informed us, he said: Khalid ibn al-Harith narrated to us, from Said ibn Abi Aruba, from Qatada, from Abu Muslim, from al-Jarud, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rebuked drinking standing."
"6418","6831 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn al-Harith narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Aruba narrated to us, from Qatada, from Abu Muslim, from al-Jarud ibn al-Mualla, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"6419","6832 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn al-Harith narrated to us, from Said, from Qatada, from Abu Muslim, from al-Jarud. And from Said, from Qatada, from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"6420","6833 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Samad narrated to us, he said: Hammam and Hisham narrated to us, both said: Qatada narrated to us, from Anas ibn Malik, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"6421","6834 - Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Khushaysh narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Abi Abd Allah narrated to us, from Qatada, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"6422","6835 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hisham al-Dastuwai narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"6423","6836 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatada, from Anas, and from Qatada, from Abu Isa al-Uswari, from Abu Said, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"6424","6837 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ismail narrated to us. 6838 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, both said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ikrima, from Abu Hurayra, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. Abu Jafar said: So a group went to disliking drinking standing, and argued with these reports. Others opposed them and saw no harm in drinking standing."
"6425","6839 - And they argued with what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed me, from Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Husayn, from his father, from his grandfather who said: Ibn Abi Talib said to me: Bring me water for ablution. So I brought it to him and he performed ablution, then he stood with the remainder of his ablution water and drank standing, and I was astonished at that. He said: Are you astonished, my son? I saw your father the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do that."
"6426","6840 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Maysara, from al-Nazzal ibn Sabra who said: I saw Ali drink the remainder of his ablution water standing. Then he said: People dislike drinking standing, and I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do what I did."
"6427","6841 - Abu Bakra narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Misar narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"6428","6842 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Warqa ibn Umar narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Zadhadhan and Maysara, from Ali, that he drank standing and it was objected to him in that. He said: If I drink standing, I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) drink standing, and if I drink sitting, I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do that."
"6429","6843 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Zadhadhan, from Ali, the like of it."
"6430","6844 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"6431","6845 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Asim al-Ahwal, from al-Shabi, from Abd Allah ibn Abbas who said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) drink while standing."
"6432","6846 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from al-Shaybani, from Amir, from Ibn Abbas who said: I handed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a bucket of Zamzam water, and he drank while standing."
"6433","6847 - Ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Asim al-Ahwal, from al-Shabi, from Ibn Abbas, the like of it."
"6434","6848 - Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Abi Farwa al-Madani narrated to us, he said: Ubayda bint Nabil narrated to us, from Aisha bint Sad, from Sad ibn Abi Waqqas that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to drink standing."
"6435","6849 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Hafs narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar who said: We used to drink while standing in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6436","6850 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim and Uthman ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to us, both said: Imran ibn Hudayr narrated to us, from Abu al-Bazri, and he is Yazid ibn Utarid, from Ibn Umar who said: We used to drink while standing, and eat while walking, in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6437","6851 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Imran ibn Hudayr, from Yazid ibn Utarid, from Ibn Umar, the like of it."
"6438","6852 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj who said: Abd al-Karim ibn Malik informed me, he said: al-Bara ibn Zayd informed me, that Umm Sulaym narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) drank while standing from a waterskin."
"6439","6853 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiya narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Karim al-Jazari narrated to us, he said: al-Bara ibn bint Anas, and he is Ibn Zayd, narrated to me, from Anas ibn Malik who said: My mother narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon her, and in her house was a hanging waterskin, and he drank from the waterskin standing."
"6440","6854 - Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Humayd, from Anas that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) drank from a hanging waterskin while standing. So in these reports is permission to drink standing, and the worthiest thing for us when two hadiths are narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and they can agree and can contradict, is to carry them upon agreement not contradiction. And what we narrated in this section from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was permission to drink standing, and in what we narrated from him in the previous section, prohibition of that. So it is possible that prohibition was not intended to remove this permission but another meaning was intended, so we looked into that."
"6441","6855 - So Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Khalid narrated to us, from Bayan, from al-Shabi who said: I only dislike drinking standing because it is an ailment. So al-Shabi reported in this the meaning for which there was prohibition, and that it was for what is feared of harm and occurrence of ailment, nothing else. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended by that prohibition compassion for his ummah and commanding them with what is their welfare in their religion and world, as he said to them: As for me, I do not eat reclining."
"6442","6856 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sahl ibn Bakkar narrated to us. 6857 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, both said: Abu Awana narrated to us, from Ruqayya, from Ali ibn al-Aqmar, from Abu Juhayfa who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: As for me, I do not eat reclining."
"6443","6858 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ali ibn al-Aqmar, from Abu Juhayfa who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say, and he mentioned the like of it."
"6444","6859 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ali ibn al-Aqmar, from Abu Juhayfa, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"6445","6860 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Misar ibn Kidam narrated to us, from Ali ibn al-Aqmar who said: I heard Abu Juhayfa say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, and he mentioned the like of it. So that is not by way of prohibiting upon them to eat so, but for a meaning in eating reclining which he feared for them."
"6446","6861 - Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, he said: al-Shabi said: He only disliked eating reclining fearing their bellies would grow large. So al-Shabi reported the meaning for which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disliked eating reclining, that it is only for what results from it of largeness of belly. Likewise what was narrated from him of prohibition of drinking standing is only for a meaning resulting from that which he disliked for it, nothing else. And there was narrated on this also from Abd Allah ibn Amr."
"6447","6862 - Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us. 6863 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, both said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Shuayb ibn Abd Allah ibn Amr, from his father who said: I never saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) eat reclining at all. So it is possible he avoided that for what al-Shabi said, and another meaning is possible in that."
"6448","6864 - For Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahia narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Jafar, from Ismail al-Awar who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to eat reclining, then Jibril, peace be upon him, descended upon him and said: Look at this servant, how he eats reclining. He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat. So it is possible this is the meaning for which he said: I do not eat reclining, for it is the act of arrogant kings and the act of non-Arabs, so he disliked that and inclined to the act of the Arabs, as was narrated from Umar."
"6449","6865 - For Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun, he said: Asim al-Ahwal narrated to us, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi who said: The letter of Umar ibn al-Khattab came to us: Be rough and coarse and live simply and be like Mad, as if you are Mad, and beware of luxury and the dress of non-Arabs. Do you not see he forbade them the dress of non-Arabs and commanded them with living like Mad, which is the rough living the Arabs know. Likewise eating reclining, they were forbidden it because it is the act of non-Arabs. As for drinking sitting, they were commanded with it fearing what it causes in their chests, and there is in that nothing of the dress of non-Arabs."
"6450","6866 - And there was narrated on permission of drinking standing from a group of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Abd al-Ala, from Bishr ibn Ghalib who said: I entered upon al-Husayn ibn Ali in his house, and he stood to a young she-camel of his and massaged its udder until when it let down milk, he called for a vessel and milked, then drank while standing, then said: O Bishr, I only did that so you know that we drink while standing."
"6451","6867 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Amir ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr who said: I saw my father drink while standing."
"6452","6868 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym, from Ali ibn Abd Allah al-Bariqi who said: I handed Ibn Umar a waterskin, and he drank from its mouth standing. And it was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he forbade drinking from the mouth of the waterskin."
"6453","6869 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Qatada, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade drinking from the mouth of the waterskin."
"6454","6870 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ikrima, from Abu Hurayra, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. So this prohibition from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was not upon prohibiting that to his ummah so that one who does it of them is disobedient to him, but for a meaning about which there is disagreement what it is."
"6455","6871 - Then Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade drinking from the mouth of the waterskin because it makes it stink. So this is its meaning."
"6456","6872 - And another meaning was narrated on that, which is what Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Layth, from Mujahid who said: He used to dislike drinking from the break of the cup and the handle of the jug, and said: They are the sitting place of Satan. So this prohibition from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was not by way of prohibition, but upon compassion from him for his ummah and mercy with them and care for them. And a group said: He only forbade that because it is the place vermin aim for, so he forbade that fearing their harm. Likewise what we mentioned from him at the start of this chapter, of his forbidding drinking standing, is not upon the prohibition by which its doer is disobedient, but for the meaning we mentioned in that. And we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) earlier in this chapter that he came to the house of Umm Sulaym and drank from a waterskin while standing from its mouth. So that shows his prohibition narrated from him in that is not upon the prohibition by which its violator must be disobedient. But it is upon prohibition from fear; when fear goes, prohibition is lifted. This with us is the meaning of these reports, and Allah knows best. And there was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) also that he forbade bending the mouths of waterskins, which is to break them and drink from their mouths."
"6457","6873 - Ismail ibn Yahya al-Muzani narrated that to us, he said: al-Shafii narrated to us, from Sufyan ibn Uyayna, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah, from Abu Said al-Khudri that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade bending the mouths of waterskins."
"6458","6874 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it. Ibn Abi Dhib said: Bending them is to break them and drink from them. So the aspect he forbade that for is the aspect for which he forbade drinking from the mouth of the waterskin."
"6459","14 - Chapter on placing one foot upon the other. 6875 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfa narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, from Jabir that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disliked a man placing one of his feet upon the other."
"6460","6876 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth informed me, from his father, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it, and he added: while lying down."
"6461","6877 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us. 6878 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, both said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"6462","6879 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: al-Mutamir narrated to us, from his father, from Khidash, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"6463","6880 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Umayya ibn Bistam narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, from Rawh ibn al-Qasim, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu Bakr ibn Hafs, from Abu Hurayra, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he forbade a man folding one of his feet upon the other. Abu Jafar said: So a group disliked placing one foot upon the other, for these reports."
"6464","6881 - And they also argued with what Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from Wasil, from Abu Wail who said: al-Ashath, Jarir ibn Abd Allah and Kab were sitting, and al-Ashath raised one of his feet upon the other while sitting. Kab ibn Ujra said to him: Put it down, for it is not fit for a human. Others opposed them and saw no harm in that, and argued with what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6465","6882 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abbad ibn Tamim, from his uncle who said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) lying on his back in the mosque, placing one of his feet upon the other."
"6466","6883 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Yaqub ibn Abi Abbad narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: al-Zuhri narrated to me, he said: Abbad ibn Tamim narrated to me, from his uncle Abd Allah ibn Zayd, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"6467","6884 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr al-Hanafi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, he said: al-Zuhri narrated to us, he said: Abbad ibn Tamim narrated to me, from his uncle, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"6468","6885 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas and Yunus narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abbad ibn Tamim, from his uncle, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"6469","6886 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"6470","6887 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abd Allah al-Majishun narrated to us. 6888 - And Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Jad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abd Allah narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab who said: Mahmud ibn Labid narrated to me, from Abbad ibn Tamim, from his uncle, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. They said: So these reports came from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with permission of what the first reports forbade. As for what they mentioned, of what they argued with from the word of Kab ibn Ujra, it was narrated from a group of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) contrary to that."
"6471","6889 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Malik and Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Said ibn al-Musayyib that Umar ibn al-Khattab and Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with them both, used to do that."
"6472","6890 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to me, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Umar, he said: Salim Abu al-Nadr narrated to me, he said: Abu Bakr, Umar and Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them, one of them would sit cross-legged with one of his feet upon the other."
"6473","6891 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Jafar narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Muhammad, from Said ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Yarbu that he saw Uthman ibn Affan do that."
"6474","6892 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab who said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz informed me, that Muhammad ibn Nawfal narrated to him that he saw Usama ibn Zayd ibn Haritha, in the mosque of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), do that."
"6475","6893 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Usama ibn Zayd al-Laythi informed me, from Nafi that he saw Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, do that."
"6476","6894 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Jabir, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad ibn Yazid who said: I saw Abd Allah lying at al-Arak placing one of his feet upon the other, while saying: Our Lord, make us not a trial for the wrongdoing people."
"6477","6895 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Imran ibn Muslim who said: I saw Anas ibn Malik sitting, having placed one of his feet upon the other. So we narrated from these eminent ones among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and this is of what cannot be clarified by reasoning, so we use in it what we used in other chapters of this book. But for what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as we described in the preceding section, and it was narrated from Kab ibn Ujra that he said: It is not fit for a human, its meaning with us, and Allah knows best, is that it is not fit for a human because of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding it, for it is not fit for a human to oppose the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then came what we mentioned in the second section of its permission, by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) doing it. So it is possible one of the two matters abrogated the other. When we found Abu Bakr, Umar and Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them, the Rightly Guided Caliphs, near to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and knowing his command, did that after him in the presence of all his Companions, and among them the one who narrated the first hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on dislike, and none of them objected, then Abd Allah ibn Masud, Ibn Umar, Usama ibn Zayd and Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with them, did it and no objector objected to them. Thereby it is established that this is what the people of knowledge are upon, of these two raised reports, and what opposed it is null for what we mentioned and clarified. And there was narrated from al-Hasan on that what shows another meaning."
"6478","6896 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Nizar al-Ayli narrated to us, he said: al-Sari ibn Yahya narrated to me, he said: Uqayl narrated to us, he said: It was said to al-Hasan: He used to dislike a man placing one of his feet upon the other? Al-Hasan said: They only took that from the Jews. So it is possible it was in the law of Musa, peace be upon him, to dislike that act, and the Jews were upon that. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded following what they were upon, for his ruling is to be upon the law of the Prophet before him, until Allah makes for him a law abrogating with his law. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded contrary to that, with permission of that act, when Allah the Mighty and Majestic permitted for him what He had prohibited upon those before him. And contrary to that was also narrated from al-Hasan."
"6479","6897 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Humayd, from al-Hasan that he used to do it, meaning placing one foot upon the other, and said: It was only disliked for him to do that before people, fearing exposure. The first aspect with me is more likely than this. Do you not see the word of Kab: It is not fit for a human. Had it been the meaning narrated from al-Hasan in this hadith, Kab would not have said that. But he only said that for his knowledge of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding, for what he was upon of following those before him, then Allah the Mighty and Majestic abrogated and Kab did not know it, so he was upon the first matter, and others knew it and returned to it and left what preceded it."
"6480","15 - Chapter on a man passing through the mosque with arrows. 6898 - Abu Bakra and Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, both said: Abu Ahmad Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Burda narrated to us, from Abu Burda, from Abu Musa, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: When one of you passes in our mosque or in our mosques, with arrows in his hand, let him hold their blades, lest he injure anyone with them. Abu Jafar said: So a group went to that there is no harm for a man to cross the mosque carrying what he wishes to carry, and argued with this hadith. Others opposed them and said: It is not fitting for anyone to enter the mosque carrying anything of that, unless he entered with it intending prayer by his entering, or if when he entered it he intends charity with it. As for entering with it intending crossing the mosque, that is disliked. And they said: It is possible the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended with what we mentioned in the hadith of Abu Musa, bringing in for charity. So we looked whether we find any reports showing that."
"6481","6899 - So Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith and al-Layth ibn Sad informed me, one adding over the other, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir who said: A man used to give charity with arrows in the mosque, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him not to pass with them except holding their blades."
"6482","6900 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, from al-Layth, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. So Jabir clarified in this hadith that those who entered with them the mosque only intended charity therein, not crossing. So this is what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted, of what is in the hadith of Abu Musa."
"6483","16 - Chapter on embracing. 6901 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama, Hammad ibn Zayd and Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, from Hanzala al-Sadusi, from Anas ibn Malik that they said: O Messenger of Allah, does one of us bow to another when we meet? He said: No. They said: Does one of us embrace another? He said: No. They said: Does one of us shake hands with another? He said: Shake hands."
"6484","6902 - Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Abu Hilal narrated to us, from Hanzala, from Anas who said: We said: O Messenger of Allah, then he mentioned the like of it. Abu Jafar said: So a group went to this and disliked embracing, among them Abu Hanifa and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them both. Others opposed them and saw no harm in it, and among those who went to that was Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on him. And part of what they argued with in that was what"
"6485","6903 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Kurayb Muhammad ibn al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Amr narrated to us, from Mujalid ibn Said, from Amir, from Abd Allah ibn Jafar, from his father who said: When we came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from the Negus, he met me and embraced me."
"6486","6904 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Muhammad al-Taymi narrated to us, he said: Abu Awana narrated to us, from al-Ajlah, from al-Shabi who said: The arrival of Jafar coincided with the conquest of Khaybar. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I do not know with which of the two I am more glad, with the conquest of Khaybar or the arrival of Jafar. Then he met him and embraced him and kissed between his eyes."
"6487","6905 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Yahya ibn Muhammad al-Shajari narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn Abbad narrated to me, he said: Ibn Ishaq informed me, from Muhammad ibn Muslim ibn Shihab al-Zuhri, from Urwa ibn al-Zubayr, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: Zayd ibn Haritha came to Madinah, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in my house, so he came to him and knocked on the door, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood to him uncovered, and by Allah I never saw him uncovered before it, and he embraced him and kissed him. And there was narrated on that from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what"
"6488","6906 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to me, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from Ghalib al-Tammar, from al-Shabi that the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when they met, shook hands, and when they came from travel, embraced."
"6489","6907 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us. 6908 - And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, both said: Shuba narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"6490","6909 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghalib narrated to us, from Umm al-Darda who said: Salman came to us and said: Where is my brother? I said: In the mosque. So he came to him, and when he saw him he embraced him. So these Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to embrace. So that shows what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of permission of embracing is later than what was narrated from him of forbidding that. By that we take, and it is the view of Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on him."
"6491","17 - Chapter on images in clothes. 6910 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Raja narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from Ali ibn Mudrik who said: I heard Abu Zura ibn Amr ibn Jarir, from Abd Allah ibn Nujayy, from his father who said: I heard Ali, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Angels do not enter a house in which is an image."
"6492","6911 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ishaq and Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, both said: Shuba narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"6493","6912 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, he said: Mughira ibn Miqsam narrated to us, he said: al-Harith al-Ukli narrated to me, from Abd Allah ibn Nujayy, from Ali, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Jibril, peace be upon him, said to me: We do not enter a house in which is a dog, nor an image, nor a statue."
"6494","6913 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Bukayr, from Kurayb, freedman of Ibn Abbas, from Ibn Abbas that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he entered the House found therein an image of Ibrahim and an image of Maryam, and said: As for them, they heard that angels do not enter a house in which is the image of Ibrahim, so why does he cast lots."
"6495","6914 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah, from Ibn Abbas, from Abu Talha that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Angels do not enter a house in which is an image."
"6496","6915 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, he said: Suhayl ibn Abi Salih narrated to us, from Said ibn Yasar, from Abu Talha, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"6497","6916 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Umayya ibn Bistam narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from Said ibn Yasar, from Zayd ibn Khalid, from Abu Ayyub, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"6498","6917 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abd Allah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Hazim, from his father, from Abu Salama, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, that Jibril, peace be upon him, said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): We do not enter a house in which is an image."
"6499","6918 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Abu Zayd ibn Abi al-Ghamr narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqba, from Nafi, from al-Qasim, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: I bought a cushion in which were images. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon me and saw it he changed, then said: O Aisha, what is this? I said: A cushion I bought for you to sit upon. He said: We do not enter a house in which are images."
"6500","6919 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: al-Awzai narrated to me, he said: Ibn Shihab narrated to me, he said: al-Qasim informed me, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon me while I was screened with a curtain of qiram in which was an image, and he tore it down, then said: The most severely punished people on the Day of Resurrection are those who imitate the creation of Allah the Mighty and Majestic."
"6501","6920 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib informed me, from al-Harith ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Kurayb, freedman of Ibn Abbas, from Usama ibn Zayd, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Angels do not enter a house in which is an image."
"6502","6921 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Jad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Mihran, from Umayr, freedman of Ibn Abbas, from Usama ibn Zayd, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he entered the Kaba and saw therein an image, and ordered me and I brought him a bucket of water, and he began striking the images with it, saying: May Allah destroy people who depict what they do not create."
"6503","6922 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Umar ibn Muhammad narrated to me that Salim ibn Abd Allah narrated to him, from his father, that Jibril said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): We do not enter a house in which is an image."
"6504","6923 - Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb said to us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Ibn al-Sabbaq, from Ibn Abbas, from Maymuna wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"6505","6924 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahia narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, he said: I asked Jabir about images in the house and about a man doing that. He said: The Messenger of Allah rebuked that."
"6506","6925 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from Umara ibn al-Qaqa, from Abu Zura who said: I entered with Abu Hurayra the house of Marwan ibn al-Hakam, and there were statues. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah the Mighty and Majestic said: And who is more unjust than one who goes to create creation like My creation. Let them create an atom, or let them create a grain, or let them create barley. Abu Jafar said: So some went to disliking taking clothes in which are images, and what of that is trodden and used, and what is worn, and they disliked its being in houses, and argued with these reports. Others opposed them and said: What of that is trodden and used, there is no harm in it, and they disliked other than that. And part of the proof for them in that was what"
"6507","6926 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Usama ibn Zayd al-Laythi informed me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his mother Asma bint Abd al-Rahman, and she was in the care of Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came from travel, and with me was a mat of mine in which was an image, and I placed it upon my recess, then he pulled it away and said: Do not cover the wall. She said: So I made it two cushions, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took them leaning upon them."
"6508","6927 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Amr informed me, from Bukayr al-Ashajj, from Rabia ibn Ata, freedman of Banu al-Azhar, that he heard al-Qasim ibn Muhammad mention from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to lean upon them."
"6509","6928 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Bakr ibn Mudar narrated to me, from Amr ibn al-Harith, from Bukayr, that Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim narrated to him, that his father narrated to him, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, that she had set up a curtain in which were images, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered and pulled it down, so she cut it into two cushions. A man in the gathering then, called Rabia ibn Ata, freedman of Banu Azhar, said: Did you hear Abu Muhammad mention that Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, said: And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to lean upon them? He said: No, but I heard al-Qasim ibn Muhammad mention that from her."
"6510","6929 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to me, he said: Muhammad ibn Abi al-Wazir narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, that she made a curtain in which were images toward the qibla. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded her, so she pulled it down and made from it two cushions, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to sit upon them."
"6511","6930 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik narrated to him, from Nafi, from Aisha Mother of the Believers, may Allah be pleased with her, that she bought a cushion in which were images. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw it, he stood at the door and did not enter, and she recognized dislike in his face. She said: O Messenger of Allah, I repent to Allah and to His Messenger, what sin did I commit? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What is the matter with this cushion? I said: I bought it for you to sit upon and recline upon. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The makers of these images will be brought forth on the Day of Resurrection and it will be said to them: Give life to what you created. Then he said: The house in which are images, angels do not enter it."
"6512","6931 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father who said: Aisha said: There was a garment in which were images, and I placed it before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he prayed, and he disliked it, or she said: and he forbade me, so I made it cushions. So the people of this view said: What is trodden, there is no harm for these reports, and what is other than what is trodden, it is that about which the first reports came. And it was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he excepted from what he forbade of images, except what was a figure in a garment."
"6513","6932 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me that Bukayr ibn al-Ashajj narrated to him: That Busr ibn Said narrated to him, that Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani narrated to them, and with Busr ibn Said was Ubayd Allah al-Khawlani, that Abu Talha narrated to him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Angels do not enter a house in which is an image. Busr said: Then Zayd ibn Khalid fell ill, and we visited him, and there we were in his house with a curtain in which were images. I said to Ubayd Allah al-Khawlani: Did you not hear him narrate to us on images? He said: He said: Except a figure in a garment, did you not hear him? I said: No. He said: Yes, he mentioned that."
"6514","6933 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Salim Abu al-Nadr, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utba who said: Abu Talha ibn Sahl fell ill, and Uthman ibn Hunayf said to me: Would you like to visit Abu Talha? I said: Yes. He said: So we came to him and entered upon him, and under him was a mat in which was an image. He said: Remove this mat and cast it from me. Uthman ibn Hunayf said to him: Did you not hear, O Abu Talha, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he forbade images? He said: Except a figure in a garment, or a garment in which is a figure? He said: Yes, but it is better for my soul, so remove it from me."
"6515","6934 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik narrated to him, from Abu al-Nadr, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it, except he said in place of Uthman ibn Hunayf: Sahl ibn Hunayf. So it is established with what we narrated that images in clothes exit from the forbidden images, and it is established that what is forbidden is images which are like what the Christians do in their churches of images in their walls, and hanging imaged clothes therein. As for what is trodden and used and spread, it exits from that, and this is the school of Abu Hanifa, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them."
"6516","6935 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Kamil narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Layth narrated to us, he said: I entered upon Salim ibn Abd Allah while he was reclining upon a red cushion in which were images. He said: I said: Is this not disliked? He said: No, only what is hung of it is disliked, and what is erected of statues, as for what is trodden, there is no harm in it. He said: Then 6936 - he narrated to me from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The makers of these images will be punished on the Day of Resurrection until they breathe the soul into them. It will be said to them: Give life to what you created. So this from the word of Salim shows what we mentioned. Then people differed after that on these images what they are. A group said: In that entered the image of everything, of what has a soul and what has no soul. They said: Because the report came in that unspecified."
"6517","6937 - And they also argued with what Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Waki and Yahya ibn Isa narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu al-Duha, from Masruq, from Abd Allah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The most severely punished people on the Day of Resurrection are the image-makers."
"6518","6938 - Abu Bakra narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, he said: Awn ibn Abi Juhayfa narrated to us, informing from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cursed the image-maker. Others opposed them and said: What of that has no soul, there is no harm in depicting it, and what has a soul, it is what is forbidden to depict. And they argued with what was narrated from Ibn Abbas."
"6519","6939 - Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Humran narrated to us, he said: Awf ibn Abi Jamila narrated to us, from Said ibn Abi al-Hasan who said: I was with Ibn Abbas when a man came to him and said: O Ibn Abbas, my livelihood is only from the work of my hand, and I make these images. Ibn Abbas said: I only narrate to you what I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever makes an image, Allah will punish him for it on the Day of Resurrection until he breathes the soul into it, and he will never breathe. He said: The man breathed heavily and his face turned yellow, and he said: Woe to you, if you refuse but to make, upon you is trees and everything in which is no soul."
"6520","6940 - Ali ibn Shayba narrated to us, he said: Qabisa narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Awf, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it. And the word of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Allah will punish him for it until he breathes the soul into it, shows the correctness of what Ibn Abbas said of this. So that shows what was forbidden to depict is what has soul in it. And there was narrated on that also, from other than Ibn Abbas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Image-makers will be punished on the Day of Resurrection, and it will be said to them: Give life to what you created."
"6521","6941 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Qanabi narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Umar narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Image-makers will be punished on the Day of Resurrection. It will be said to them: Give life to what you created."
"6522","6942 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"6523","6943 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Ayyub, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"6524","6944 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us, from Qatada, from Ikrima, from Abu Hurayra who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever makes an image will be punished on the Day of Resurrection until he breathes the soul into it, and he will never breathe. So the meaning of these reports is the meaning of what we narrated from Ibn Abbas. And there was narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on that also what shows this meaning."
"6525","6945 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wuhazi narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, he said: When Mujahid came to Kufa, I and my father came to him, and he narrated to us from Abu Hurayra who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Jibril came to me and said: O Muhammad, I came to you last night and could not enter the house, for in the house was a statue of a man, so command the statue, let its head be cut off until it becomes like the shape of a tree."
"6526","6946 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Mujahid, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: Jibril (peace be upon him) asked permission to enter upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Enter. He said: How can I enter when in your house there is a curtain containing images of horses and men? Either cut off their heads, or make it a mat to be spread on the floor, for we, the company of angels, do not enter a house in which there are images. When images were permitted after cutting off their heads, such that if cut from a living being it would not survive, that proved the permissibility of depicting what has no soul, and that images of what has no living counterpart are excluded from what was forbidden in the reports we mentioned in this chapter. And there has also been narrated from Ikrimah in this chapter what (follows)."
"6527","6947 - Muhammad ibn al-Nu'man narrated to us, he said: Abu Thabit al-Madani narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from a man, from Ikrimah, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The image is the head, so everything that has no head is not an image. And in Jibril's (peace be upon him) saying to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the hadith of Abu Hurayrah: either make it a mat or cut off their heads, is proof that the use of what contains such images was only permitted by spreading it flat. If one says: in the hadith of Abu Talhah, there was a curtain with pictures in his house, and he did not consider it included in what he heard from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The angels do not enter a house in which there is an image, because he heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: except what is a figure on a garment. It is said to him: as for the curtain, it was the action of Abu Talhah, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) may not have informed him that the excepted garment was the curtain, and the curtain may also be among what was excepted. Since this is possible, and in the hadith of Mujahid from Abu Hurayrah from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) there is what we described, we know that the permitted garments are those spread out like mats, not hanging or worn garments, and this is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them."
"6528","6948 - Musa ibn al-Mubarak narrated to me, he said: Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Qattan narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Ali al-Ju'fi narrated to us, from Za'idah, from Mundhir, from al-Rabi' ibn Khuthaym, he said: None of you should say: I seek Allah's forgiveness and repent to Him, then return (to sin), so it becomes his lie and a sin; but let him say: O Allah, forgive me and turn to me in mercy."
"6529","6949 - And among their proof for that was what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar al-Hawdi (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd Allah al-Wasiti narrated to us, from Ibrahim al-Hajari, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abd Allah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Repentance from sin is that a man repents from the sin, then never returns to it."
"6530","6950 - This is the description of repentance, and no one is safe in this except the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), for he is infallible, and that is why he used to say what was narrated from him, of what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Khattab ibn Uthman and Haywah ibn Shurayh narrated to us, they said: Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from al-Zubaydi, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bakr ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham, from Abu Hurayrah, that he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: I repent in a day one hundred times. And Anas said: he only said: seventy times."
"6531","6951 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ayyub ibn Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Uways narrated to me, from Sulayman, from Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Atiq and Musa ibn Uqbah, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: I seek Allah's forgiveness and repent to Him in a day more than seventy times."
"6532","6952 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Salamah ibn Rawh narrated to us, he said: Uqayl narrated to us, he said: al-Zuhri narrated to us that Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham informed him that Abu Hurayrah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then he mentioned the same."
"6533","6953 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, from Yunus, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"6534","6954 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ja'far narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Uqbah informed me, from Abu Ishaq, he narrated to him, from Abu Burdah ibn Abi Musa, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I seek Allah's forgiveness and repent to Him in a day one hundred times."
"6535","6955 - Rabi' the muezzin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, he said: Ziyad ibn al-Mundhir narrated to us, he said: Abu Burdah ibn Abi Musa narrated to us, he said: al-Agharr al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us raising his hands, saying: O people, seek your Lord's forgiveness, then repent to Him, for by Allah, I seek Allah's forgiveness and repent to Him in a day one hundred times. They said: this the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say because he is protected from sins, while others should not say it because they are not protected from returning to what they repented from. Others disagreed with them and saw no harm in a man saying: I repent to Allah, the Mighty and Majestic. And among their proof was what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6536","6956 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Musa ibn Uqbah informed me, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Whoever sits in a gathering in which his idle talk is abundant, then says before standing: Glory be to You, our Lord, there is no god but You, I seek Your forgiveness and repent to You, he is forgiven for what was in that gathering of his."
"6537","6957 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Matar narrated to us, from Thabit, from Anas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The expiation of the gathering is: Glory be to You, O Allah, and with Your praise, I seek Your forgiveness and repent to You."
"6538","6958 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah and Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, they said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Ibn al-Had narrated to me, from Isma'il ibn Abd Allah ibn Ja'far, he said: It reached me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no person in a gathering who says when he wants to stand: Glory be to You, O Allah, and with Your praise, there is no god but You, I seek Your forgiveness and repent to You, except he is forgiven for what was in that gathering."
"6539","6959 - He said: Yazid ibn Khusayfah narrated to us this hadith, saying: al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid narrated to me thus, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6540","6960 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah and Fahd narrated to us, they said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Ibn al-Had narrated to me, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Zurarah, from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not rise from a gathering except saying: Glory be to You, O Allah, my Lord, and with Your praise, there is no god but You, I seek Your forgiveness and repent to You. I said to him: O Messenger of Allah, how often you say these words when you rise! He said: No one says them when rising from his gathering except he is forgiven for what was in that gathering. This too is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and it is the preferable of the two views in our opinion, for Allah commanded it in His Book, saying: So turn in repentance to your Creator, and saying: Turn to Allah in sincere repentance. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded it in the reports we mentioned, so we permitted it and differed with Abu Ja'far in what he held, as we mentioned at the start of this chapter. If one says: Allah only commanded them to repent, and repentance is abandoning sins and not returning to them, not merely saying: we have repented - likewise is narrated regarding His saying: Turn to Allah in sincere repentance."
"6541","6961 - He mentioned what Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ziyad al-Makhzumi narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, he said: Simak narrated to us, from al-Nu'man ibn Bashir, he said: I heard Umar say: Sincere repentance is that a man avoids whatever he used to do, repents to Allah from it, then never returns to it."
"6542","6962 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Simak, from al-Nu'man, from Umar, the same. This is the description of the repentance Allah commanded in His Book. As for their saying: we repent to Allah, it is nothing of this. It was said to them: even if as you say, we did not permit them to say: we repent to Allah while believing they will return to what they repented from. Rather we permitted it on the basis that they mean abandoning the sin they fell into, with no desire to return to any of it. When they say that with this belief in their hearts, they are rewarded for it. Whoever returns afterward to any of those sins, it is a sin he committed, and it does not nullify the reward recorded for his earlier saying and belief. As for one who says: I repent to Allah while believing he will return to what he repented from, he is by that saying a transgressor, punished for it, for he has lied about Allah in what he said. But if he says it while believing in abandoning the sin he fell into and resolved never to return to it, he is truthful in his saying, rewarded for his truthfulness, Allah willing. And it is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Remorse is repentance."
"6543","6963 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, he said: Ziyad ibn Abi Maryam informed me, from Abd Allah ibn Ma'qil, he said: I entered with my father upon Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud, and my father said to him: Did you hear the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Remorse is repentance? He said: Yes."
"6544","6964 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, from Malik, from Abd al-Karim, from a man, from his father, from Ibn Mas'ud, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"6545","6965 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Ziyad ibn Abi Maryam and Ibn al-Jarrah, from Abd Allah ibn Mughaffal, then he mentioned with his chain the same."
"6546","6966 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Haytham ibn Jamil narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Abd al-Karim, from Ziyad, who is not Ibn Abi Maryam, then he mentioned with his chain the same."
"6547","6967 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Karim narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Mughaffal, similar. This Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made remorse repentance. This proves that whoever says: I repent to Allah from such and such sin, while remorseful for that sin he committed, is doing well and rewarded for that saying."
"6548","6968 - Chapter: Weeping over the dead. Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas informed me, from Abd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Jabir ibn Atik, that Atik ibn al-Harith ibn Atik, the grandfather of Abd Allah ibn Abd Allah, his mother's father, informed him that Jabir ibn Atik informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to visit Abd Allah ibn Thabit, found him overcome, called out to him and he did not answer. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: We are overcome regarding you, O Abu al-Rabi'. The women cried out and wept, and Ibn Atik began quieting them, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Leave them; when the obligation occurs, let no weeping woman weep. They said: O Messenger of Allah, what is the obligation? He said: When he dies. Abu Ja'far said: Some people held weeping over the dead disliked, arguing with this hadith and with what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The dead is punished by his family's weeping over him."
"6549","6969 - Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn al-Azraqi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-Ward narrated to us, he said: I heard Ibn Abi Mulaykah say: When Umm Aban, daughter of Uthman ibn Affan, died, I attended with the people and sat before Abd Allah ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) and Abd Allah ibn Abbas. The women wept. Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Will you not forbid these women from weeping? I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The dead is punished by some of his family's weeping over him. Ibn Abbas said: Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) used to say that. I went out with Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) until when we were at al-Bayda, there was a caravan. He said: O Ibn Abbas, who is the caravan? I went and it was Suhayb and his family. I returned and said: Commander of the Believers, it is Suhayb and his family. When we entered Madinah and Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) was struck, Suhayb sat weeping over him, saying: O my beloved, O my companion. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Do not weep, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The dead is punished by some of his family's weeping over him. He said: That was mentioned to A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), and she said: By Allah, you do not narrate this hadith from liars, but hearing can err, and in the Qur'an there is what suffices you: No bearer of burdens bears another's burden. But the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah increases the disbeliever's punishment by some of his family's weeping over him."
"6550","6970 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, and he mentioned similar, except he did not mention the story of Suhayb. They said: since the dead is punished by his family's weeping over him, their weeping over him was disliked for them. Others disagreed, saying: there is no harm in weeping over the dead if it is weeping without disobedience, without foul speech or wailing."
"6551","6971 - And they argued with what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Sa'id ibn al-Harith al-Ansari, from Abd Allah ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: Sa'd ibn Ubadah fell ill, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to visit him with Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, and Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud. When he entered upon him, he found him unconscious and said: He has passed away. They said: No, by Allah, O Messenger of Allah. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wept. When the people saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) weeping, they wept, and he said: Do you not hear that Allah does not punish for the tear of the eye nor the grief of the heart, but He punishes for this - and he pointed to his tongue - or shows mercy."
"6552","6972 - Ahmad ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, he said: I heard Sufyan say: Ibn Ajlan narrated to us, from Wahb ibn Kaysan, from Abu Hurayrah, that Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) saw a woman weeping over a dead person and forbade her. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Leave her, O Abu Hafs, for the soul is afflicted, the eye weeps, and the covenant is recent."
"6553","6973 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd al-Laythi narrated to me, from Nafi', from Abd Allah ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by the women of Banu Abd al-Ashhal weeping for their dead on the day of Uhud. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: But Hamzah has no weeping women. So the women of the Ansar came weeping for Hamzah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) woke and said: Woe to them, they have not yet returned after their passing; let them return and not weep over any dead after today."
"6554","6974 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Asim ibn Ubayd Allah, from al-Qasim, from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kissing Uthman ibn Maz'un after his death, with his tears flowing onto his beard. In these reports we mentioned is the permissibility of weeping over the dead, for it does not harm them nor cause their punishment. Had it not been so, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not have wept nor permitted weeping, and would have forbidden it. If one says: in the hadith of Ibn Umar there is abrogation, his saying: and let them not weep over any dead after today. It is said: there is no proof in that; his saying may mean those dead whom they had wept over until that time, for that weeping had relieved their grief. And there is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the explanation of the weeping he meant to forbid."
"6555","6975 - What Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abd Allah ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Ata, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took my hand, and I went with him to his son Ibrahim while he was breathing his last. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took him and placed him in his lap until his soul departed, then laid him down and wept. I said: O Messenger of Allah, do you weep while you forbid weeping? He said: I did not forbid weeping, but I forbade two foolish wicked voices: a voice at amusement, play, and Satan's flutes, and a voice at calamity: slapping faces and tearing garments; and this is mercy - whoever does not show mercy is not shown mercy. O Ibrahim, were it not a true promise, a true word, and that our last will join our first, we would grieve for you more than this; we are grieved for you; the eye weeps, the heart grieves, and we do not say what angers the Lord. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) thus explained the weeping he forbade in the earlier hadiths: weeping accompanied by loud voice, face-slapping, and tearing garments; and clarified that other weeping done from mercy is different. As for what we mentioned from Umar about: the dead is punished by his family's weeping - A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) denied it, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah increases the disbeliever's punishment in his grave by some of his family's weeping over him. And that weeping may be what he had enjoined in his life, for the people of ignorance used to enjoin their families to do so after death, so Allah punishes him in his grave for a cause he initiated in life, carried out after his death."
"6556","6976 - And this hadith is narrated from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) in other wording: Rabi' the muezzin narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi al-Zinad informed me, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that she said: May Allah forgive Abu Abd al-Rahman ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him); he says: the dead is punished by the living's weeping. By Allah, that is only a misunderstanding of Abd Allah ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), may Allah forgive him; Allah says: No bearer of burdens bears another's burden. It is only that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a Jew's grave and said: You weep over him while he is punished in his grave - he says: for his deed. So A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) informed that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only reported that disbeliever is punished in his grave for his deed while his family weep over him, and Allah has prevented one soul bearing another's burden. This proves no dead is punished in his grave by a living's weeping he did not command in life, per Jabir's hadith from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf: what the disliked weeping is - that accompanied by slapping and (tearing)."
"6557","6977 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Amir ibn Sa'd, he said: I visited Qarazah ibn Ka'b, Abu Mas'ud al-Ansari, and Thabit ibn Zayd, and with them were slave girls singing. I said: Do you do this while you are the companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? They said: If you wish to listen, else go away, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted amusement at weddings and weeping over the dead. If one says: it is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): the dead is punished in his grave by his family's wailing over him."
"6558","6978 - And he mentioned what Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Ubayd, Abu al-Hudhayl al-Ta'i narrated to us, from Ali ibn Rabi'ah, he said: Wailing was performed over Qarazah ibn Ka'b, so al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah addressed and said: What is with wailing in this ummah? I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Lying about me is not like lying about anyone; whoever lies about me deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire; and whoever is wailed over is punished for what is wailed over him. It was said to him: this in our view - and Allah knows best - concerns wailing they enjoined upon their families, done after them by their bequest in life, so they are punished for it, and Allah knows best."
"6559","6979 - Chapter: Narrating poetry, is it disliked or not? Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman and Muhammad ibn Sulayman al-Baghandi narrated to us, they said: Khallad ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from Amr ibn Hurayth, from Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: That one's belly be filled with pus is better for him than being filled with poetry."
"6560","6980 - Muhammad ibn Isma'il al-Sa'igh narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Yunus ibn Jubayr, from Muhammad ibn Sa'd, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: That one's belly be filled with pus until it overflows is better for him than being filled with poetry."
"6561","6981 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith narrated to us, from Shu'bah, then he mentioned with his chain the same."
"6562","6982 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, from Shu'bah, then he mentioned with his chain the same, except he did not say: until it overflows."
"6563","6983 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: I heard Hanzalah, he said: I heard Salim ibn Abd Allah say: I heard Abd Allah ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) narrate from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"6564","6984 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Ja'd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ja'far al-Razi narrated to us, from Asim, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"6565","6985 - Muhammad ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Muslim narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same, adding: until it overflows."
"6566","6986 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Shimashah, from Awf ibn Malik, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: That one's belly, from his navel to his throat, be filled with pus churning like a waterskin is better for him than being filled with poetry."
"6567","6987 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Sulayman al-A'mash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: That one's belly be filled with pus is better for him than being filled with poetry. Abu Ja'far said: Some people disliked narrating poetry, arguing with these reports. Others disagreed, saying: there is no harm in narrating poetry without vileness. They said: what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerns a specific kind of poetry."
"6568","6988 - They mentioned what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ayyash informed me, from Muhammad ibn al-Sa'ib, from Abu Salih, he said: It was said to A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her): Abu Hurayrah says: That one's belly be filled with pus is better for him than being filled with poetry. A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: May Allah have mercy on Abu Hurayrah; he memorized the beginning of the hadith but not its end; the polytheists used to lampoon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said: That one's belly be filled with pus is better for him than being filled with poetry lampooning the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"6569","6989 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Abu Ubayd narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid narrate from al-Sharqi ibn al-Qutami, from Mujalid, from al-Sha'bi, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: That one's belly be filled with pus is better for him than being filled with poetry - meaning the poetry with which the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was lampooned. They said: reports permitting poetry are also narrated."
"6570","6990 - Among them is what Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami narrated to us, he said: Ma'n ibn Isa narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to me, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered in the year of the Conquest, he saw women striking horses' faces with veils, and smiled, saying: O Abu Bakr, how did Hassan ibn Thabit say? So Abu Bakr recited. Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us thus, while Arabic scholars hold the first verse otherwise, so its rhyme matches the next verse. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Enter it from where he said."
"6571","6991 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Zuhri narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Some poetry is wisdom."
"6572","6992 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from al-Miqdam ibn Shurayh, from his father, he said: I said to A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her): Did the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recite any poetry? She said: Yes, from the poetry of Ibn Rawahah, and he would sometimes say this verse."
"6573","6993 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ma'in narrated to us, he said: Abdah ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: Hassan sought the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permission to lampoon the polytheists. He said: What about my lineage among them? He said: I shall draw you out from them as a hair is drawn from dough."
"6574","6994 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sulayman al-Taymi narrated to us, from Mujalid ibn Sa'id, from al-Sha'bi, he said: We were sitting in the courtyard of the Ka'bah, I think he said: with some companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), reciting poetry to one another. Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr stopped by us and said: In the sanctuary, around the Ka'bah, reciting poetry? One of them said: O Ibn al-Zubayr, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only forbade poetry in which women are approached and the dead reviled. So the poetry about which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said what we mentioned at the start may be the poetry forbidden in this hadith."
"6575","6995 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Qays ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Abidah, from Abd Allah, and from al-A'mash, from Umarah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from Abd Allah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Some poetry is wisdom."
"6576","6996 - Ibn Abi Dawud, Fahd, and Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, they said: Abd Allah ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ghaniyyah narrated to us, from his father, from Asim, from Zirr, from Abd Allah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Some poetry is wisdom."
"6577","6997 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Marwan, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad ibn Abd Yaghuth, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Some poetry is wisdom."
"6578","6998 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Abi al-Wazir narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, then he mentioned with his chain the same, except he said: from Abd Allah ibn al-Aswad ibn Abd Yaghuth."
"6579","6999 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the same, except he said: from Abd Allah ibn al-Aswad ibn Abd Yaghuth."
"6580","7000 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Numayr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from Mujalid, from al-Sha'bi, from Jabir, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who will defend the honor of the believers? Ka'b said: I. Ibn Rawahah said: I. He said: You compose poetry well. Hassan ibn Thabit said: Then I. He said: Lampoon them, for the Holy Spirit will aid you against them."
"6581","7001 - Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: Abu Ibrahim al-Tarjuman narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from A'ishah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) placed a pulpit for Hassan ibn Thabit in the mosque, upon which he recited poetry."
"6582","7002 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us, then he mentioned the same as Ibn Abi Dawud's hadith before this, from Ibn Numayr, from Ibn Fudayl."
"6583","7003 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us. (Continuation in the next report.)"
"6584","7004 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj and Abd Allah ibn Raja narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: Adi ibn Thabit informed me, he said: I heard al-Bara say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say to Hassan: Lampoon them - or: attack them in verse - and Jibril is with you."
"6585","7005 - Muhammad ibn Amr narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq al-Shaybani, from Adi, then he mentioned with his chain the same."
"6586","7006 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Adi ibn Thabit narrated to me, meaning he said: I heard al-Bara ibn Azib say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say to Hassan ibn Thabit: The Holy Spirit will remain with you as long as you lampoon the polytheists."
"6587","7007 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, that Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) passed by Hassan while he was reciting in the mosque of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) rebuked him. Hassan turned to him and said: I used to recite in it when one better than you was present, so Umar left him. Hassan said to Abu Hurayrah: O Abu Hurayrah, did you not hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: O Hassan, answer on behalf of the Messenger of Allah; O Allah, support him with the Holy Spirit? He said: O Allah, yes."
"6588","7008 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Ma'mar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, that Hassan, then he mentioned the same, except the saying: I used to recite in it when one better than you was present, which he did not mention."
"6589","7009 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to me that he heard Hassan ibn Thabit asking Abu Hurayrah to recite, then he mentioned the same."
"6590","7010 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Wahid ibn Anbasah al-Qurashi narrated to us, he said: my grandfather Anbasah narrated to me, from Yunus ibn Ubayd, from al-Hasan, from al-Aswad ibn Sari', who was a poet, that he said: O Messenger of Allah, shall I not recite praises with which I praised my Lord? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Your Lord loves praise, and he did not ask him for more than that."
"6591","7011 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, from al-Aswad ibn Sari', the same, except he said: so I began reciting to him."
"6592","7012 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Mushir narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn Abi al-Rijal narrated to me, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to me, he said: Hisham ibn Urwah narrated to us, from his father, from A'ishah, she said: Abd Allah ibn Rawahah spoke and did well, then Ka'b spoke and did well, then Hassan spoke and cured, and was asked for more cure."
"6593","7013 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Numayr narrated to us, he said: Abdah ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Ya'qub ibn Utbah, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) confirmed Umayyah ibn Abi al-Salt in his poetry, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He spoke truth, and said (a verse). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He spoke truth."
"6594","7014 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: Abu Ma'shar al-Barra narrated to us, from Sadaqah ibn Taysalah, he said: Ma'n ibn Tha'labah narrated to me, and the tribe after him, he said: A'sha al-Mazini narrated to me, he said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and recited to him (verses). He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) began saying: And they are the worst overcomers for whoever overcomes."
"6595","7015 - al-Hasan ibn Abd Allah ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: al-Haytham ibn Jamil narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Simak, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Some poetry is wisdom."
"6596","7016 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Qays narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Abidah, from Abd Allah. (Continuation in the next report.)"
"6597","7017 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Qays narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Umarah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from Abd Allah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"6598","7018 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Ya'la ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Amr ibn al-Sharid, from his father, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked me to recite the poetry of Umayyah ibn Abi al-Salt, so I recited to him; each time I recited a verse, he said: More, until I recited one hundred rhymes. He said: Umayyah ibn Abi al-Salt almost accepted Islam."
"6599","7019 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Mu'alla ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from Umar ibn al-Hakam, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, he said: al-Aqra' ibn Habis said to a youth among them: Stand and mention your merit and your people's merit. So he stood and spoke. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Hassan, answer him. So he spoke (verses). Since these continuous reports permit composing poetry, it is established that what was forbidden in the first reports is not because poetry is disliked, but for a meaning in a specific kind of poetry. Some interpreted these first reports otherwise, saying: had the lampoon of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) been meant, mention of fullness would be meaningless, for little or much of that is disbelief; rather fullness indicates a meaning in fullness itself. He said: in our view, it concerns poetry that fills the belly, leaving no Qur'an, glorification, or anything else. As for one whose belly contains Qur'an along with poetry, he is not one whose belly is filled with poetry, and is excluded from his saying: That one's belly be filled with pus is better for him than being filled with poetry. 7020 - Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: I heard Ubayd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn A'ishah explain this hadith thus, and I heard Ibn Abi Imran and Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz mention that from Abu Ubayd as well."
"6600","7021 - Chapter: The sneezer is blessed, how should he respond to one who blesses him. Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Warqa narrated to us, from Mansur, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Khalid ibn Urfutah, he said: We were with Salim ibn Ubayd when a man among the people sneezed and said: Peace be upon you. Salim said: Upon you and upon your mother; what has peace to do with this matter. Then he went a while and said to the man: Did what I said to you weigh on you? He said: I wished you had not mentioned my mother with good or otherwise. He said: While we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), a man among the people sneezed and said: Peace be upon you. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Upon you and upon your mother; when one of you sneezes, let him say: Praise be to Allah, Lord of the worlds, or: in every circumstance; and let them reply to him: May Allah have mercy on you; and let him reply to them: May Allah forgive you."
